#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""account — which login a session runs on: an ordered registry of accounts, walked by one pick.

    account pick [--kind K] [--provider P] [--bound] [--repo PATH] [--ship KEY] [--json]   the first account with room; exit 3 if none
    account ships [--release KEY]                               the ships pinned to a login here; --release unpins one
    account orca-sync [--off]                                   every Claude login in Orca → a row in the walk (on by default; --off records new ones off), its dir wired, its VS Code profile; every Codex login Orca uses → a codex row
    account orca on|off LABEL | orca default on|off             an Orca login in or out of the walk, on this Mac, at once (an agent --orca reports: its card); the default for new ones
    account vscode LABEL                                        a VS Code profile LABEL whose Claude Code panel runs on that login
    account limits [LABEL | --all [--parked] [--orca]] [--repo PATH] [--json] [--cached]  an account's windows, plan, Fable; --parked: the registry's parked rows too; --orca: every other agent Orca reads limits for
    account allowed PATH                                        exit 1 if this dir's row denies the repo at PATH (an Orca dir no row names: its login's row, else ORCA_ROW's list), or PATH is another tenant's
    account which                                               the label of the dir this process runs under
    account env LABEL                                           the start env: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=… (or nothing)
    account run LABEL [CLAUDE ARGS…]                            exec claude on that login here; refuses a denied cwd or a disabled row
    account exhausted --window W --resets-at T [--session S]    record a locked window (bin/statusline calls it)
    account check                                               per row: login, links, settings, probe age, cooldowns
    account add LABEL [--no-login]                              make a Claude row's config dir, then its /login
    account setup [--host PEER] [--no-record]                   build this Mac (or the peer) from the logins file
    account doctor [--local] [--json]                           both Macs against the logins file, a fix per problem

Design: docs/record/multi-account-design-2026-10-02.md (D1–D7, the owner 2026-10-02); orchestration
build step 4b. The owner: "We should protect the primary one, and offload what we can to secondary
accounts. We should have a list, in prioritized order."

Where things are (regatta-modules B9). The registry is the config dir's `lighthouse/accounts.json`
(an overlay links its own; labels name accounts, so it is personal; nothing secret in it), else
$ACCOUNTS_FILE. The logins file is `lighthouse/logins.json` (else $ACCOUNT_LOGINS). The owner's
private vault, the repo a work login is refused and whose refusal marks a row as an employer's, is
`lighthouse/vault` (a repo key under the projects folder). A peer Mac is a `<host>:<ssh alias>` line
of `wharf-runs/ssh`. The core comes through lib/find_core.py, and a missing core is an ImportError
with a traceback, which account_guard takes as no answer and fails closed on. A peer module is
found through `common.tool()`: wharf's fork_claude_session.py (a Terminal window; without it the
command is printed) and worktree_allow.py (without it that check is skipped), plumb's usage-report
(without it no usage column) — the design's G16, G18. The overlay's bootstrap.sh (the core's
`legacy` checkout) is named where a fix is its; without one the fix says so.

The registry: {"accounts": [{label, provider, home, rules, primary?, enabled?}, …],
"capabilities": {provider: [kind, …]}}. Order is the walk order for free work; the one row with
`primary: true` runs account-bound work (Remote Control, connectors, Artifact, Chrome, routines,
every VS Code panel session). A step is free unless the caller says --bound.

A Claude account is a Claude Code config directory with its own `/login`: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
isolates the login (keychain entry `Claude Code-credentials-<sha256(dir)[:8]>`), `.claude.json`,
settings and agent view; `projects/` and `sessions/` are links back to the default dir so
transcripts, memory and the live registry stay one (D3). The default dir is special in one way: it
must run with CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR *unset* — set to the default dir, Claude Code looks for a
hash-suffixed keychain entry and a .claude.json inside it and finds itself logged out. So its env
is empty.

Limits are read without switching anything: a headless `claude -p` answering one `get_usage`
control request — no model call, $0, about 4 s (probed 2026-10-03). It runs with
`--setting-sources "" --no-session-persistence --strict-mcp-config`, so no user hook fires and
no transcript is written (R8, checked the same day). Cached 60 s in <home>/usage-probe.json;
--cached never probes (hooks use it). One retry on a failed probe, then the last reading of any
age; with none, the row is "unknown" and skipped — except the primary, which pick takes at once
rather than hand its work to a later row (the Pro fallback has no Fable) or block it. Never a loop.

Exhaustion (D6): <home>/exhaustion.jsonl, one record per locked window. bin/statusline writes it
when a window's used_percentage reaches 100; a probe writes it when a window reads 100 or carries
a locked_reason (the design's "severity warning" was dropped: the probe's own `is_active` marks
the binding window at any level, 68% included, 2026-10-03). Pick skips the account until that
window resets (cooldown) and asks it for MARGIN more points of 5-hour headroom per record in the
last MARGIN_DAYS days, up to MARGIN_CAP.

The logins file (work-account design E1, docs/work-account-2026-10-06.md) is what the owner would
otherwise carry in mind: per label, the e-mail, plan, who pays and which Macs carry a dir — kept
on a shared drive, never git (it names e-mails, not secrets). {"logins": {label: {email, plan,
org_type, paid_by, macs, note}}, "share": {"design": [{email, access}]}} — `share` is
hooks/design_route.py's, kept on every write. An e-mail and plan are read back from a login and
written here, never typed; `macs: []` records that no Mac carries a dir; `"enabled": false` on a
row of the registry is what parks it (a work login was parked 2026-10-15 to 2026-10-18; the Pro
fallback was parked 2026-10-07 and back, after the primary, since 2026-10-16). Only the laptop
writes it: two Macs writing one Drive file within seconds lose one write (the core's bin/secrets
header), so the always-on Mac's leg runs with --no-record.

A row may carry `"repos": {"deny": [key, …]}` (work-account D3: a work login denies the vault and
the CV repo): keys of repos.json — a path under the projects folder — matched against the repo a
path resolves to through `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` (a worktree of the vault is the vault),
or by path prefix for a folder that is no repo. `pick --repo` and `limits --repo` skip a row that
denies the repo; `allowed` is the guard hook's question (E4). A path in no repo — the projects
folder itself, the home folder — is allowed: the list says where sessions start, it is no data
boundary (section 2 of the doc) — bar, for `allowed`, a path that holds a denied repo
(holds_denied, since 2026-10-18): Claude Code loads the default dir's CLAUDE.md as such a folder's
project memory, imports included.
A repo in a scope whose `login` names a label (repos.json's scopes, read through the core's
`scope_login`; knowledge-layers D3, 2026-10-20) is picked onto that row alone: `pick --repo` skips
every other row, waits (exit 3) when that one cannot run rather than move a confidential node's
run to another seat, and a ship pinned elsewhere moves to it.

Tenants (multi-tenancy, the owner 2026-10-20: "instead of banning [an employer's] projects from
[another employer's login], send their sessions to a separate account"). The registry may say
`"tenants": {name: {"repos": [key or glob, …]}}` and a row `"tenant": name`: a tenant's repos run on
its own rows only. Every other row is refused them, as if they were on its deny list. That includes
the default dir, which otherwise denies nothing. Only rows refuse, never the tenant's own, so pick
routes rather than bans: `pick --repo` (dispatch, pickup, autofinish, a ship) walks past every
other row to the tenant's login and waits (exit 3) when that one has no room, never falling back to
another seat. A tenant's own row is not confined to its repos: its own deny list says where else it
may run (an organisation's seat wants the vault on it). Entries are repo keys as in a deny list,
or a glob (`acme-*`; `*` spans `/`) matched against the key and, when git cannot answer, the path,
so a repo cloned later is the tenant's the day it lands. With no reading of the tenant login's
limits, pick runs it there rather than nothing, as the primary's rule has it: no other row may. `holds_denied` ignores tenancy: a folder holding a
tenant's repo loads nothing of it. The guard (account_guard) asks `allowed` on the default dir too
while any tenant exists, and the refusal names the way in: the login's VS Code profile (`vscode
LABEL`), or `cc X` in a Terminal. `doctor` checks that VS Code ties each tenant repo's folder, and
each workspace that opens one, to a profile of the tenant's login.
The tenants file (`lighthouse/tenants.json`, since 2026-10-22: the owner keeps the list in the vault,
not in the repo that holds the registry) says the same in one place, `{name: {"repos": […], "logins":
[label, …]}}`: written, it is the whole of tenancy, and the registry's `tenants` and rows' `tenant` are
ignored. Each good read is copied beside it (`.tenants.json.last`), and a link that reads nothing (the
drive behind it away) holds by that copy, never failing open: that would let every seat, another
employer's included, into a tenant's repos. Only with no copy either is tenancy off, said on stderr
(not under --json). To end tenancy, write `{}`: a deleted file or link leaves the copy holding.

Codex rows (E7) read their limits from Codex's own `codex app-server`: `account/rateLimits/read`
on the row's CODEX_HOME, no model call, $0, about 1 s (probed 2026-10-16, figures equal to Orca's).
Its usage-probe.json and exhaustion.jsonl are written into that Codex home, beside Codex's own files.
Known gaps (reviews of 2026-10-17, left on purpose): a probe on the live default Codex home may
refresh its token beside the desktop app (safe by Codex's guarded re-read, read from the binary,
never seen); a home re-signed to another account keeps the old login's cache and exhaustion log; a
managed Codex home is Orca's source path, unseen (a wrong one fails loudly: "no dir");
wharf-runs' bin/pickup's refusal of a Codex pick has no test (only a seed with `provider: codex`
reaches it). An Orca login no registry row names gets ORCA_ROW's deny list, the core's
`regatta/orca-deny`; the other repos an employer login is refused are refused by their registry
rows (the owner, 2026-10-18). A new employer login added through Orca needs its row in the
registry first when its deny list is longer than ORCA_ROW's.
A plan has the windows it has (prolite: the week alone), and a window a plan lacks is no limit.
`pick --provider codex` answers with a Codex row, but nothing starts Codex yet: `run` and bin/pickup
start claude only and refuse a Codex pick; the readings feed `limits` and the desktop widget. Orca's Codex logins become rows as its Claude ones do (`orca-sync`, orca_codex_sync).
Every other agent Orca can sign in to — Antigravity, Cursor — is Orca's to read: `limits --all --orca`
adds one report-only row per agent Orca reads limits for (the owner, 2026-10-20: "add antigravity
from orca … automate this"), so one signed in there reaches the widget with no change here. Those
rows are no registry rows: never walked, picked or probed, and gone when Orca says the agent is
signed out; Orca's other `unavailable` (a CLI too old, a provider switched off) and its errors keep
the last reading (ORCA_SEEN) with its age and Orca's word, as does an Orca that cannot be read.
Stdlib only, Python 3.9.

A row may say `"via": "orca"` (docs/orca-accounts-2026-10-16.md; the owner, 2026-10-16: "add one
more account … through Orca instead of our own mechanism"): Orca holds that login — it signs in
(`orca account add --agent claude`), builds the account's own config dir under its data (syncing
settings.json, hooks and most links from the default dir itself — Orca's source, review
2026-10-16), and signs in again from its Accounts UI. Orca's list names no dir (its `summarize`
returns id, email, auth fields only), so `orca-sync` finds it from ground truth: the dir under
ORCA_DATA whose `.claude.json` is signed in as that entry's e-mail, read back, never predicted.
The dir is this Mac's fact, so it lives in ORCA_FILE beside the default dir, never in the shared
registry; the other Mac has none and walks past the row. Until a sync the row has no home here and
pick, env and run refuse it ("not in Orca here"). Everything else — the probe, limits, the walk,
deny lists — is the same for it: a login Orca holds is still a config dir.

A ship stays on one login (same doc): `pick --ship KEY` returns the login KEY was pinned to while
that row is enabled, here, on its own login and allowed in the repo — waiting (exit 3) rather than
moving when it has no room, except the primary with unreadable limits, which runs as plain pick
has it. A ship with no pin gets the walk's pick and is pinned to it; `--no-pin` leaves that to
the caller (`ships --pin KEY LABEL` once its worker started). Pins live in SHIPS, per Mac, written
under a lock, and go after PIN_DAYS unused or on `ships --release KEY` (the ship landed).
"""
import argparse
import contextlib
import datetime as dt
import fcntl
import fnmatch
import functools
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

HOME = Path.home()
DEFAULT_DIR = HOME / ".claude"
CONFIG = Path(os.environ.get("REGATTA_CONFIG") or HOME / ".config" / "regatta").expanduser()
REGISTRY = Path(os.environ.get("ACCOUNTS_FILE") or CONFIG / "lighthouse" / "accounts.json")
# A registry named by ACCOUNTS_FILE (a suite's fixture) has its own tenants file beside it, never the
# live key: a fixture must not take the Mac's tenancy, nor write its last good copy (review 2026-10-22).
TENANTS = Path(os.environ.get("TENANTS_FILE")
               or (REGISTRY.with_name(REGISTRY.name + ".tenants") if os.environ.get("ACCOUNTS_FILE")
                   else CONFIG / "lighthouse" / "tenants.json"))
BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
CACHE_S = 60          # a probe answers for this long (R4: one probe per minute per account)
PROBE_TIMEOUT = 30    # two tries per row: pickup's 150 s covers two rows (review 2026-10-03)
SAME_RESET = 300      # seconds: two exhaustion records whose resets are this close are one
MARGIN = 10           # D6: points of 5-hour headroom an exhaustion asks for…
MARGIN_DAYS = 7       # …for this long…
MARGIN_CAP = 30       # …stacking to at most this
WINDOWS = ("five_hour", "seven_day")


def config_lines(name):
    """The config dir's `<name>`, its non-blank lines ([] when absent or unreadable): read without
    the core, since the constants below are."""
    try:
        return [l.strip() for l in (CONFIG / name).read_text(encoding="utf-8-sig").splitlines() if l.strip()]
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return []


# the ssh alias per peer Mac, as wharf-runs' bin/pickup and bin/dispatch have it: `<host>:<alias>`
PEERS = dict(l.split(":", 1) for l in config_lines("wharf-runs/ssh") if ":" in l)
VAULT = (config_lines("lighthouse/vault") or [""])[0]   # the owner's private vault, a repo key (header)
SHIPS = DEFAULT_DIR / "account-ships.json"        # {ship key: {label, at}}: the login each ship stays on
PIN_DAYS = 14         # a pin older than this is no ship any more and goes at the next write
ORCA_FILE = Path(os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_ORCA_FILE") or DEFAULT_DIR / "account-orca.json")   # orca-sync's read-back, this Mac's: {label: {home, orca_id, email, enabled?}, _default?}; hooks/common.py orca_file()
ORCA_SEEN = Path(os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_ORCA_SEEN") or DEFAULT_DIR / "account-orca-limits.json")   # limits --orca's last reading per agent, this Mac's: {label: {at, limits, enabled?}}
ORCA_DATA = Path(os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_ORCA_DATA") or HOME / "Library/Application Support/orca")
ORCA_APP_CLI = "/Applications/Orca.app/Contents/Resources/bin/orca"
# What a secondary dir shares with the default dir by link (D3, section 2e). Never .claude.json, the
# keychain entry, settings.json (rename-writes turn a link into a copy, R1), daemon/ or jobs/.
LINKS = ("projects", "sessions", "skills", "plugins", "agents", "rules", "CLAUDE.md")
# CLAUDE.md is linked, not copied: Claude Code never rewrites it, and a copy drifts silently from
# the one the owner edits (code review 2026-10-03).
# An employer login's CLAUDE.md links the work file instead (the owner, 2026-10-18, answering
# docs/orca-hosts-2026-10-17.md F5: "work files only"): the owner's working-style files, never the
# private ones. "Employer" is any row refused the vault — a work login by its own deny list, an
# Orca login by ORCA_ROW's — so the rule follows the deny lists, never a list of names.
# The overlay's bootstrap.sh writes the work file. Orca rewrites a profile's CLAUDE.md from its own one-line stub
# at each setup but leaves a symlink alone ("user-owned", claude-profile-sharing.ts), so a link is
# what survives there.
WORK_MEMORY = "CLAUDE-work.md"
ORCA_MEMORY_STUB = "@~/.claude/CLAUDE.md\n"   # what Orca 1.4.2xx writes into a profile's CLAUDE.md


def overlay_key_missing(rel, env=None):
    """ImportError when the overlay is installed (the config dir's `regatta/overlay` or `legacy`) and its key `rel` is
    not written, nor named by `env`: a guard reading an absent key as "nothing to deny" fails open,
    so this one fails closed, as the core does for its own keys (B2b; B9 review)."""
    if ((CONFIG / "regatta" / "overlay").exists() or (CONFIG / "legacy").exists()) and not (env and os.environ.get(env)) and not (CONFIG / rel).exists():
        raise ImportError(f"{rel} missing in {CONFIG} — write it with the overlay's bootstrap.sh --config")


def employer(a):
    """True when the row is refused the vault (`lighthouse/vault`): its CLAUDE.md gets the work file.
    No vault named, no employer row — bar where the overlay is installed: there it is an error."""
    if not VAULT:
        overlay_key_missing("lighthouse/vault")
    return bool(VAULT) and bool(denied(a, projects_dir() / VAULT, routes=False))


def projects_dir():
    """The core's projects folder (`regatta/projects`, else `projects` in the home folder)."""
    try:
        return core().PROJECTS
    except ImportError:
        return HOME / "projects"


def core_hooks():
    """The regatta core's hooks dir, in lib/find_core.py's order; ImportError without one — a
    traceback, which account_guard reads as no answer and fails closed on (header). Never "": on
    sys.path that is the cwd, and a common.py there would answer for the core (B9 review)."""
    sys.path.insert(0, str(BIN.parent / "lib"))
    import find_core
    found = next((str(c) for c in find_core.candidates() if (c / "common.py").is_file()), None)
    if not found:
        raise ImportError("no regatta core in any of lib/find_core.py's places")
    return found


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def core():
    """The core's `common` (ImportError when there is none); found once per run."""
    sys.path.insert(0, core_hooks())
    import common
    return common


def overlay(*rel):
    """A path in the overlay's checkout (the core's `legacy`), or None without one."""
    try:
        old = core().legacy_root()
    except ImportError:
        return None
    return old.joinpath(*rel) if old else None


def bootstrap_cmd():
    """The overlay's bootstrap.sh by path, for a fix line; a placeholder without an overlay."""
    b = overlay("bootstrap.sh")
    return str(b) if b and b.is_file() else BOOTSTRAP_PLACEHOLDER


BOOTSTRAP_PLACEHOLDER = "<your overlay's bootstrap.sh>"


def remote_account():
    """This tool as the peer runs it: the path sessions know (the overlay's while it holds a shim
    there, else this file), written from the home folder so the peer's own home applies."""
    old = overlay("bin", "account")
    mine = old if old and old.is_file() else Path(__file__).resolve()
    try:
        return "$HOME/" + str(mine.relative_to(HOME))
    except ValueError:
        return str(mine)


def trust_argv(a, *extra):
    """bin/trust-projects for the row's dir: an employer row's also answers No, ahead, to the
    external-imports dialog, the one way the default dir's CLAUDE.md private imports could still
    load there (its CLAUDE.md links the work file, so that file loads as the home folder's project
    memory, its imports behind that dialog — review 2026-10-18)."""
    return [str(BIN / "trust-projects"), *extra] + (["--no-external-imports"] if employer(a) else [])


def link_target(a, n):
    """What the row's dir links `n` to: DEFAULT_DIR's own, bar CLAUDE.md on an employer row."""
    return DEFAULT_DIR / (WORK_MEMORY if n == "CLAUDE.md" and employer(a) else n)


def point_memory(a, home):
    """Points home/CLAUDE.md where link_target says, replacing only a link of ours or Orca's stub
    (never a file someone wrote); → the line to print, or None when it already points there."""
    p, target = Path(home) / "CLAUDE.md", link_target(a, "CLAUDE.md")
    if not target.is_file():   # never a link to nothing: such a session would load no me/ file at all
        return f"  ⚠ fail  {p}: {target} does not exist — a plain {bootstrap_cmd()} run writes it"
    if p.is_symlink() and os.readlink(p) == str(target):
        return None
    ours = p.is_symlink() and os.readlink(p) in (str(DEFAULT_DIR / "CLAUDE.md"), str(DEFAULT_DIR / WORK_MEMORY))
    try:
        stub = not p.is_symlink() and p.is_file() and p.read_text() == ORCA_MEMORY_STUB
    except OSError:
        stub = False
    try:
        if p.exists() or p.is_symlink():
            if not (ours or stub):
                return f"  ⚠ left  {p}: not a link of ours nor Orca's stub — point it at {target} by hand"
            tmp = p.with_name(f".CLAUDE.md.{os.getpid()}.relink")   # per process: two runs never share it
            if tmp.is_symlink() or tmp.exists():
                tmp.unlink()
            tmp.symlink_to(target)
            os.replace(tmp, p)   # one rename: never a moment without the file
        else:
            p.symlink_to(target)
    except OSError as e:
        return f"  ⚠ fail  {p}: {e}"
    return f"  linked  {p} → {target}"


def home_memory_excluded(a, home, fix=True):
    """An employer dir's own settings.json excludes the default dir's CLAUDE.md from its memory →
    None, or the line to print. Its CLAUDE.md links the work file, so the default dir's CLAUDE.md is
    no longer the same file: Claude Code loads it as the home folder's project memory, and its
    private imports raise "Allow external CLAUDE.md file imports?" in every new worktree — where an
    unattended run sat until it timed out (2026-10-18 afternoon, both Macs; the fix proved on the
    always-on Mac's work login by another session, through the bulletin). claudeMdExcludes in this
    dir's own settings.json, never the default dir's: the CLI applies it to user memory too. Orca
    re-copies the default dir's settings.json over a profile's at each setup, so orca-sync re-applies it."""
    if not employer(a):
        return None
    p, want = Path(home) / "settings.json", str(DEFAULT_DIR / "CLAUDE.md")
    try:
        cfg = json.loads(p.read_text()) if p.exists() else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return f"  ⚠ fail  {p}: {e}"
    have = cfg.get("claudeMdExcludes") if isinstance(cfg.get("claudeMdExcludes"), list) else []
    if want in have:
        return None
    if not fix:
        return f"  ⚠       {p}: no claudeMdExcludes for {want}"
    if p.is_symlink():   # never through a link: in the default dir's settings.json it would drop the primary's own memory
        return f"  ⚠ left  {p}: a link ({os.readlink(p)}) — give the dir its own settings.json first"
    try:
        write_json(p, {**cfg, "claudeMdExcludes": have + [want]})
    except OSError as e:
        return f"  ⚠ fail  {p}: {e}"
    return f"  excluded {want} in {p}"


class Short(Exception):
    """No account may run the step; args[0] lists (label, why)."""


def now():
    return float(os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_NOW") or time.time())


def claude_bin():
    return os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_CLAUDE") or shutil.which("claude") or "claude"


def logins_path():
    """The logins file: ACCOUNT_LOGINS, else the config dir's lighthouse/logins.json (an overlay
    links it into a shared drive), else None when neither exists."""
    if os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_LOGINS"):
        return Path(os.environ["ACCOUNT_LOGINS"])
    p = CONFIG / "lighthouse" / "logins.json"
    return p if p.exists() or p.is_symlink() else None


def load_logins():
    """→ (path or None, {label: entry}). An absent file is an empty dict; an unreadable one exits."""
    p = logins_path()
    if not p or not p.is_file():
        return p, {}
    try:
        return p, json.loads(p.read_text()).get("logins") or {}
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        sys.exit(f"account: {p} is not readable JSON: {e}")


def save_logins(p, logins):
    """Writes `logins` back; every other top-level key is kept as it was (`share`, which
    hooks/design_route.py reads: who a new design artifact is shared with, 2026-10-10)."""
    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    try:
        doc = json.loads(p.read_text()) if p.is_file() else {}
    except OSError as e:  # a write that cannot read what it would overwrite would drop `share`
        sys.exit(f"account: {p} is not readable now ({e}); nothing written")
    except ValueError:
        doc = {}
    doc = doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else {}
    doc["logins"] = logins
    # Through the link: the config key is a link into the shared drive, and os.replace() on the
    # link itself would swap it for a local file the other Mac never sees (B9 review).
    real = p.resolve()
    tmp = real.with_name(real.name + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, real)


def this_mac():
    if os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_HOST"):
        return os.environ["ACCOUNT_HOST"]
    return core().host_name()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- registry

def load_registry():
    try:
        reg = json.loads(REGISTRY.read_text())
    except OSError:
        overlay_key_missing("lighthouse/accounts.json", "ACCOUNTS_FILE")
        return {"accounts": [], "capabilities": {}}
    except ValueError as e:
        sys.exit(f"account: {REGISTRY} is not valid JSON: {e}")
    synced = read_json(ORCA_FILE, {})
    sys.path.insert(0, core_hooks())
    from common import account_rows  # the registry plus Orca's logins, on by default (one rule for the hooks too)
    # parked = `enabled: false` in the registry itself, read before account_rows folds in Orca's
    # `orca off` switches: only these does `limits --all --parked` bring back (review 2026-10-18)
    parked = {a.get("label") for a in reg.get("accounts") or [] if isinstance(a, dict) and a.get("enabled") is False}
    reg["accounts"] = account_rows(reg.get("accounts"), HOME)
    for a in reg.get("accounts") or []:
        if a.get("via") == "orca":  # Orca's dir, read back by orca-sync on this Mac; unsynced, nowhere
            got = (synced.get(a.get("label")) or {}).get("home")
            a["home"] = Path(got).absolute() if got else (HOME / ".orca-unsynced" / str(a.get("label"))).absolute()
            a["synced"] = bool(got)
        elif a.get("home"):
            a["home"] = Path(os.path.expanduser(a["home"])).absolute()
        else:  # a row with no home is nowhere — never the cwd, which `which` and `allowed` would match
            a["home"] = (HOME / ".no-home" / str(a.get("label"))).absolute()
        a.setdefault("provider", "claude")
        a.setdefault("enabled", True)
        a["parked"] = a.get("label") in parked
        a.setdefault("rules", {})
    mark_same_logins(reg)
    apply_tenants(reg)
    return reg


def apply_tenants(reg):
    """Tenancy (header): the tenants file when written, else the registry's `tenants` {name: {repos:
    [key or glob]}} and a row's `tenant: name`. Each row
    gets `routed` {entry: {tenant, logins}} — the other tenants' repos, which it may not run — and the
    registry `tenants` {name: [entry]}. A malformed entry is dropped, never a crash: the guard reads this."""
    raw = reg.get("tenants")
    if tenants_file():   # written, the tenants file is the whole of tenancy (header)
        raw = read_tenants_file()
        for a in reg.get("accounts") or []:
            a.pop("tenant", None)
        for n, t in raw.items():
            logins = t.get("logins") if isinstance(t, dict) else None
            logins = [logins] if isinstance(logins, str) else logins   # one label, as a row's `tenant` was
            for a in reg.get("accounts") or []:
                if isinstance(logins, list) and a.get("label") in logins:
                    a["tenant"] = n
    raw = raw if isinstance(raw, dict) else {}
    # `repos` must be a list: a string would split into one-letter entries, and a `*` among them routes
    # every repo; a number would crash every command (code review 2026-10-20). An entry is a key under
    # the projects folder, never absolute (Path.glob refuses one, and no repo key starts so).
    # Each entry is stored normalised ("acme-*/", "./acme-*" → "acme-*"): route_of re-matches the key
    # denied() returned against these, and an unstripped "acme-*/" there let the default dir in (review 2026-10-21).
    reg["tenants"] = {n: [e for e in (tenant_entry(r) for r in (t.get("repos") if isinstance(t.get("repos"), list) else [])) if e]
                      for n, t in raw.items() if isinstance(n, str) and isinstance(t, dict)}
    for a in reg.get("accounts") or []:
        a["routed"] = routes_for(reg, a)


def tenants_file():
    """True when the tenants key is written: a file, or a link, read or not (a dangling one is not "absent"),
    or its last good copy — bootstrap.sh --config removes a link whose target is away, and that must not
    end tenancy either (code review 2026-10-22). Ending it is writing `{}`."""
    return TENANTS.is_symlink() or TENANTS.exists() or tenants_last().is_file()


def tenants_last():
    """The last good read of the tenants file, kept beside it: what holds while its link reads nothing."""
    return TENANTS.with_name("." + TENANTS.name + ".last")


def read_tenants_file():
    """The tenants file's object, copied to tenants_last() whenever it reads. When it reads nothing (the
    drive behind a link away) that copy holds: failing open would let every seat, another employer's
    included, into a tenant's repos (review 2026-10-22). Neither readable: {} — tenancy off — said on
    stderr, except under --json, which dispatch parses with stderr merged in."""
    last = tenants_last()
    try:
        got = json.loads(TENANTS.read_text())
        if not isinstance(got, dict):
            raise ValueError("no object")
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        try:
            got = json.loads(last.read_text())
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            got = None
        if isinstance(got, dict):
            return got
        if "--json" not in sys.argv:
            print(f"account: {TENANTS} unreadable ({e}), and no last good copy — tenancy is off until it reads", file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    try:
        text = json.dumps(got, sort_keys=True)
        if not last.is_file() or last.read_text() != text:
            tmp = last.with_name(f"{last.name}.{os.getpid()}")
            tmp.write_text(text)
            os.replace(tmp, last)
    except OSError:
        pass   # a config dir that takes no write: the file still reads, only the fallback is lost
    return got


def tenants_source():
    """Where tenancy is read from, by its key's name, for the lines that name it."""
    return TENANTS.name if tenants_file() else "accounts.json tenants"


def no_login(name):
    """→ (why, fix) for a tenant no enabled login is marked for, in the form tenancy was written in."""
    if tenants_file():
        return (f"no enabled login is {name}'s in {TENANTS.name}", f"add its login's label to {name}'s \"logins\" in {TENANTS}")
    return (f"no enabled login has tenant {name!r} in {REGISTRY.name}", f"add \"tenant\": \"{name}\" to its login's row")


def tenant_entry(r):
    """A tenant repo entry as a key: "/" and a leading "./" stripped; None for a non-string, an empty one
    or an absolute one (Path.glob refuses one, and no repo key starts so). account_guard keeps a copy."""
    if not isinstance(r, str) or r.startswith(("/", "~")):
        return None
    while r.startswith("./"):
        r = r[2:]
    return r.strip("/") or None


def own_tenant(reg, a, path):
    """True when path is a repo of row a's own tenant: there it is the one row that may run it, so an
    unreadable reading runs the step rather than nothing, as the primary's does (review 2026-10-20)."""
    t = a.get("tenant")   # a list or object here would be unhashable: no tenant, never a crash (review 2026-10-21)
    entries = (reg.get("tenants") or {}).get(t) if isinstance(t, str) else None
    return bool(entries and path and denied({"routed": dict.fromkeys(entries)}, path))


def routes_for(reg, a):
    """{entry: {tenant, logins}} for every tenant repo entry row `a` is not the tenant of."""
    out = {}
    for name, repos in (reg.get("tenants") or {}).items():
        if a.get("tenant") == name:
            continue
        logins = [r["label"] for r in reg.get("accounts") or [] if r.get("tenant") == name
                  and r.get("enabled", True) and r.get("provider", "claude") == "claude"]
        for e in repos:
            out.setdefault(e, {"tenant": name, "logins": logins})
    return out


def is_glob(e):
    return any(c in e for c in "*?[")


def read_json(p, default):
    try:
        v = json.loads(Path(p).read_text())
        return v if isinstance(v, type(default)) else default
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return default


def write_json(p, v):
    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = p.with_name(f"{p.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")  # two writers never share a temp file
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(v, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, p)


def via_orca(a):
    return a.get("via") == "orca"


def row(reg, label):
    for a in reg["accounts"]:
        if a["label"] == label:
            return a
    sys.exit(f"account: no account {label!r} in {REGISTRY}")


def is_default(a):
    return a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"] == DEFAULT_DIR.absolute()


def env_of(a):
    """What a start on this account sets. Empty for the default dir (see the header)."""
    if a["provider"] == "codex":
        return {"CODEX_HOME": str(a["home"])}
    return {} if is_default(a) else {"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": str(a["home"])}


def start_env(a):
    e = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k != "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"}
    e.update(env_of(a))
    return e


def claude_json(a):
    return HOME / ".claude.json" if is_default(a) else a["home"] / ".claude.json"


def login_of(a):
    """→ ((accountUuid, organizationUuid), email) of the dir's stored login, from its .claude.json;
    (None, None) if unknown. Limits belong to the seat, so one account in two orgs is two keys.
    The file, not `claude auth status`: that has no uuid and costs ~1 s a row, too dear for hooks."""
    try:
        d = json.loads(claude_json(a).read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None, None
    o = d.get("oauthAccount") if isinstance(d, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(o, dict) or not o.get("accountUuid"):
        return None, None
    return (o["accountUuid"], o.get("organizationUuid")), o.get("emailAddress")


def mark_same_logins(reg):
    """A second dir signed in to a login another row already has is no second account: its limits
    are that account's, so spilling to it spends the same week. Mark it `same_as` (the primary
    keeps the login; otherwise the earlier row) — `judge` refuses it and `check` flags it. Found
    2026-10-05: the Pro fallback's dir had been logged in as the primary's address, and pick read
    the primary's 57% as the fallback's. A parked row is judged too, after every enabled one, so it never takes a login from
    one — else `limits --all --parked` draws one week as two cards (review 2026-10-18)."""
    seen = {}
    rows = sorted((a for a in reg["accounts"] if (a["enabled"] or a.get("parked")) and a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"].is_dir()),
                  key=lambda a: (not a["enabled"], not a.get("primary")))
    for a in rows:
        uid, email = login_of(a)
        if not uid:
            continue
        if uid in seen:
            a["same_as"] = (seen[uid]["label"], email)
        else:
            seen[uid] = a


def seat_of(a):
    """organizationType from the dir's .claude.json (claude_max, claude_pro, …) — the seat type
    the work-account design records and never routes on (limit 6)."""
    try:
        return (json.loads(claude_json(a).read_text()).get("oauthAccount") or {}).get("organizationType")
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None


def login_line(a, peer=None):
    """The command that signs this dir in. For a peer it is the `ssh -t` form: a plain ssh has no
    TTY for the code paste, `claude` is not on a non-login ssh PATH, and the keychain is locked to
    ssh there, so the login lands in <dir>/.credentials.json (work-account P1)."""
    if via_orca(a):
        return orca_login_line(a)
    env ="".join(f"{k}={shlex.quote(v)} " for k, v in env_of(a).items())
    if peer:
        return f"ssh -t {peer} {shlex.quote(env + claude_bin() + ' auth login')}"
    return f"{env}claude auth login"


def relogin_line(a):
    # the browser authorizes whichever claude.ai account it is signed in to — likely how the
    # duplicate arose — so the line says which one to be in
    if via_orca(a):
        return orca_login_line(a)
    env = "".join(f"{k}={shlex.quote(v)} " for k, v in env_of(a).items())
    return f"{env}claude auth login, in a browser signed in as {a['label']}'s account (a private window)"


def orca_login_line(a):
    if a.get("provider") == "codex":
        return (f"sign {a['label']} in again in Orca's Accounts screen (Codex), or `CODEX_HOME={shlex.quote(str(a['home']))} codex login`; "
                f"then {BIN / 'account'} orca-sync")
    return (f"sign {a['label']} in again in Orca's Accounts screen, or `orca account add --agent claude` in a "
            f"browser signed in as {a['label']}'s account (a private window); then {BIN / 'account'} orca-sync")


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=256)  # one git call per path, not one per row (review 2026-10-06)
def repo_of(path):
    """The repo key a path belongs to: its main checkout relative to the projects folder ("notes",
    "studio/pipeline"), through git-common-dir so a worktree is its repo; None outside the projects
    folder or outside git."""
    p = Path(path).expanduser().absolute()
    projects = projects_dir().resolve()
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(p), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        common = Path(r.stdout.strip()) if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() else None
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        common = None
    if common is None:
        return None
    top = common.parent if common.name == ".git" else common
    try:
        return str(top.resolve().relative_to(projects))
    except ValueError:
        return None


def denied(a, path, routes=True):
    """→ the repo key if this row's deny list names the repo at path — or, with `routes`, a tenant's
    repo the row is not the tenant of (header) — else None. A glob entry matches repo keys only."""
    deny = list(((a.get("repos") or {}).get("deny")) or []) + (list(a.get("routed") or {}) if routes else [])
    if not deny or not path:
        return None
    key = repo_of(path)
    if key is not None and any(key == d or (is_glob(d) and fnmatch.fnmatchcase(key, d)) for d in deny):
        return key
    p = Path(path).expanduser().absolute().resolve()
    try:
        rel = p.relative_to(projects_dir().resolve()).parts
    except ValueError:
        rel = ()
    for d in deny:  # a folder that is no git repo (the CV folder, P6) still is its path
        # A glob by path too, only when git cannot answer, so such a git never opens a tenant's repo
        # (review 2026-10-20); a key git gave that no glob matches stays unmatched (review 2026-10-21).
        if is_glob(d) and key is None:
            n = len(d.strip("/").split("/"))
            if len(rel) >= n and fnmatch.fnmatchcase("/".join(rel[:n]), d.strip("/")):
                return "/".join(rel[:n])
        # then as a folder, so a literal name holding "[" or "*" still refuses its path, as before globs
        root = (projects_dir() / d).resolve()
        if p == root or root in p.parents:
            return d
    return None


def route_of(a, key):
    """→ {tenant, logins} when repo `key` (a value denied() returned) is off row `a` by tenancy, else None."""
    for e, t in (a.get("routed") or {}).items():
        if key == e or (is_glob(e) and fnmatch.fnmatchcase(key, e)):
            return t
    return None


def routed_off(a, path):
    """→ (key, {tenant, logins}) when path is a tenant's repo row `a` may not run, else None: the one
    refusal the default dir has (its own deny list binds pick only, as before)."""
    d = denied({"routed": a.get("routed")}, path)
    t = d and route_of(a, d)
    return (d, t) if t else None


def use_line(t):
    """How to start a tenant repo's session on its own login: what the guard and run print."""
    if not t["logins"]:
        return no_login(t["tenant"])[0]
    prefix = (config_lines("lighthouse/cc-prefix") or [""])[0]
    ways = []
    for label in t["logins"]:
        short = label[len(prefix):]
        cc = f"`cc {short}`" if label.startswith(prefix) and len(short) == 1 else f"`{BIN / 'account'} run {label}`"
        ways.append(f"VS Code profile {label}, or {cc} in a Terminal")
    return "open it in " + "; or ".join(ways)


def off_line(a, key):
    """The guard's line for repo `key` off row `a`: ' is off ' is what account_guard blocks on, and a
    tenant's line ends in the way to its own login, which the guard passes on as it is."""
    t = route_of(a, key)
    if t:
        return (f"{key} is off {a['label']}: it is {t['tenant']}'s, which runs on "
                f"{', '.join(t['logins']) or 'no login'} only ({tenants_source()}) — {use_line(t)}")
    return None


def off_reason(a, key):
    """Why row `a` may not run repo `key`: its tenant's route, else its deny list (pick's skip line)."""
    t = route_of(a, key)
    if t:
        return f"{key} is {t['tenant']}'s: it runs on {', '.join(t['logins']) or 'no login'} only (tenants)"
    return f"{key} is on its deny list"


def scope_login_of(path):
    """→ (scope, login) when the repo at `path` is in a scope whose `login` names the account its
    runs start on (the core's registry reader; knowledge-layers D3, 2026-10-20), else None. A
    confidential node's pass on the walk's first row would run on another employer's seat. A
    folder that is no git repo is its path, as in denied(). A registry file that does not read →
    (its reason, "") — a login no row has, so every pick waits rather than guess (the /finish
    review of S1, 2026-10-22)."""
    if not path:
        return None
    keys = [repo_of(path)]
    try:
        rel = Path(path).expanduser().absolute().resolve().relative_to(projects_dir().resolve()).parts
        keys += ["/".join(rel[:i]) for i in range(len(rel), 0, -1)]
    except ValueError:
        pass
    try:
        c = core()
        if getattr(c, "registry_unreadable", lambda: False)():
            return ("the registry does not read", "")
        name = next((n for n in (c.scope_of_key(k) for k in keys if k) if n), None)
        login = c.scope_login(name) if name else None
    except Exception:
        return None
    return (name, login) if login else None


def holds_denied(a, path):
    """→ the key of a denied repo that lies inside `path` (path is the home or the projects folder,
    say), else None. Claude Code reads every ancestor's .claude/CLAUDE.md — the default dir's is the
    home folder's — and follows its imports when they sit inside the cwd (2.1.302's memory loader,
    read in its source 2026-10-18), so a session started there on an employer login would load the
    private imports after all."""
    deny = ((a.get("repos") or {}).get("deny")) or []
    if not deny or not path:
        return None
    p = Path(path).expanduser().absolute().resolve()
    for d in deny:
        root = (projects_dir() / d).absolute()
        if p in root.parents or p in root.resolve().parents:
            return d
    return None


def current_home():
    """The config dir this process runs under — what statusline and tier_check are part of."""
    return Path(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or DEFAULT_DIR).expanduser().absolute()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- limits

def epoch(v):
    if v is None or isinstance(v, (int, float)):
        return v
    try:
        return dt.datetime.fromisoformat(str(v).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def probe(a):
    """One get_usage control request on this account's login → the limits dict, or None."""
    if a["provider"] == "codex":
        return probe_codex(a)
    if a["provider"] != "claude":
        return None
    req = {"type": "control_request", "request_id": "u1",
           "request": {"subtype": "get_usage", "skip_behaviors": True}}
    argv = [claude_bin(), "-p", "--input-format", "stream-json", "--output-format", "stream-json",
            "--verbose", "--model", "haiku", "--setting-sources", "", "--no-session-persistence",
            "--strict-mcp-config"]
    env = start_env(a)
    for k in ("CLAUDECODE", "CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT", "CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID"):
        env.pop(k, None)  # a probe from inside a session is still its own process
    for _ in range(2):  # one retry, never a loop
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(argv, input=json.dumps(req) + "\n", capture_output=True, text=True,
                               timeout=PROBE_TIMEOUT, env=env, cwd=str(a["home"] if a["home"].is_dir() else HOME))
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            continue
        for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
            try:
                m = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            resp = (m.get("response") or {}) if m.get("type") == "control_response" else {}
            if resp.get("subtype") == "success" and resp.get("request_id") == "u1":
                return parse_usage(resp.get("response") or {})
    return None


def codex_bin():
    return os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_CODEX") or shutil.which("codex") or "codex"


def codex_rpc(home, calls, timeout=15):
    """{id: result} from Codex's JSON-RPC app-server on CODEX_HOME=home: initialize, then each
    (method, params) in calls as ids 2, 3, …; whatever answered within the timeout. An error reply
    counts as answered (as {"error": …}), so a signed-out home costs no timeout. The server exits on
    stdin's end without answering, so stdin stays open until every call answered; then it is closed
    and the server let go by itself (it holds sqlite files in the home), killed only if it lingers.
    The pipe is read raw into a line buffer of our own: select() on a buffered readline misses a
    second reply that came in the same read (review 2026-10-16). A probe on a live home (the default
    Codex home, beside the desktop app) may refresh its token: Codex re-reads auth.json under a guard before any
    refresh ("Skipping token refresh because auth changed after guarded reload", codex-cli 0.162.1's
    strings, 2026-10-16), so a running Codex takes the new token rather than reuse the spent one —
    read from the binary, never seen happen."""
    import select
    env = {**os.environ, "CODEX_HOME": str(home)}
    try:
        # --disable plugins: closing stdin early aborts Codex's plugin sync, which leaves an empty
        # .tmp/git-* dir in the home on every probe (review 2026-10-16: three a run, never cleaned)
        p = subprocess.Popen([codex_bin(), "app-server", "--disable", "plugins"], stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                             stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, bufsize=0, env=env, cwd=str(home))
    except OSError:
        return {}
    got, want, buf = {}, set(range(2, 2 + len(calls))), b""
    try:
        msgs = [{"id": 1, "method": "initialize", "params": {"clientInfo": {"name": "account", "version": "1"}}},
                {"method": "initialized"}]
        msgs += [dict({"id": n, "method": m}, **({"params": pr} if pr is not None else {}))
                 for n, (m, pr) in enumerate(calls, 2)]
        p.stdin.write("".join(json.dumps(m) + "\n" for m in msgs).encode())
        end = time.time() + timeout
        fd = p.stdout.fileno()
        while want - set(got) and time.time() < end:
            if not select.select([fd], [], [], max(0.0, end - time.time()))[0]:
                break
            chunk = os.read(fd, 65536)
            if not chunk:
                break
            *lines, buf = (buf + chunk).split(b"\n")
            for line in lines:
                try:
                    m = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                # a reply carries result or error; a message with a method is the server's own request
                if isinstance(m, dict) and m.get("id") in want and ("result" in m or "error" in m):
                    got[m["id"]] = m["result"] if "result" in m else {"error": m.get("error")}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    finally:
        try:
            p.stdin.close()
            p.wait(timeout=3)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            p.kill()
            p.wait()
    return got


def parse_codex(rl, acct=None):
    """account/rateLimits/read → the limits dict probe() gives for Claude. A window is named by
    its length (≤ a day → five_hour, else seven_day), not by primary/secondary: prolite's primary
    is its week (2026-10-16)."""
    r = (rl or {}).get("rateLimits") or {}
    who = (acct or {}).get("account") or {}
    out = {"plan": r.get("planType") or who.get("planType"), "locked": [], "fable": None, "kinds": [],
           "fable_week": None, "fable_week_resets": None, "email": who.get("email")}
    for w in WINDOWS:
        out[w], out[w + "_resets"] = None, None
    # The login's own bucket: `rateLimits` and the multi-bucket view's `codex` entry. Another limit id
    # is a per-model quota alias (the schema's normalModelSlug), which parks that model, not the login
    # — as Fable's week does for Claude — so it feeds no window; spend control anywhere is the
    # account's and locks it (reviews 2026-10-17)
    by_id = {k: b for k, b in ((rl or {}).get("rateLimitsByLimitId") or {}).items() if isinstance(b, dict)}
    buckets = [r] + ([by_id["codex"]] if "codex" in by_id else [])
    for x in [b.get(k) for b in buckets for k in ("primary", "secondary")]:
        # the schema lets windowDurationMins be null: a window of unknown length is named nothing
        if not isinstance(x, dict) or x.get("usedPercent") is None or not x.get("windowDurationMins"):
            continue
        w = "five_hour" if x["windowDurationMins"] <= 24 * 60 else "seven_day"
        if out[w] is not None and out[w] >= x["usedPercent"]:
            continue  # two windows of one class: the fuller one binds
        out[w], out[w + "_resets"] = x["usedPercent"], epoch(x.get("resetsAt"))
        if w not in out["kinds"]:
            out["kinds"].append(w)
        if x["usedPercent"] >= 100 and w not in out["locked"]:
            out["locked"].append(w)
    # "clients must not infer recovery from percentages" (the app-server's schema): a limit the
    # server says is reached, in any bucket, or spend control, locks every window the plan has
    if (any(b.get("rateLimitReachedType") for b in buckets)
            or any(b.get("spendControlReached") for b in [r] + list(by_id.values()))
            or (rl or {}).get("ordinaryUsageAllowed") is False):
        out["locked"] = sorted(set(out["locked"]) | {w for w in WINDOWS if out[w] is not None})
    return out


def probe_codex(a):
    """One account/rateLimits/read on this row's CODEX_HOME → the limits dict, or None. One retry,
    but not after an error reply: a signed-out home says so at once and again."""
    for _ in range(2):
        got = codex_rpc(a["home"], [("account/rateLimits/read", None), ("account/read", {})])
        if "error" in (got.get(2) or {}):
            return None
        if 2 in got:
            return parse_codex(got[2], got.get(3))
    return None


def fable_of(u):
    """True when the usage answer carries a weekly window scoped to Fable (the seat can run it),
    False when it carries `limits[]` without one, None when there is no `limits[]` to read
    (work-account E2; the primary's answer has it, P11)."""
    lims = limits_list(u)
    if not isinstance(lims, list):
        return None
    return fable_window(lims) is not None


def limits_list(u):
    """The `limits[]` list of a usage answer, wherever this shape keeps it, or None."""
    # rate_limits.limits in the get_usage answer (probed 2026-10-06); the /usage cache in
    # .claude.json keeps the same list under utilization.limits (P11)
    lims = (u.get("rate_limits") or {}).get("limits")
    if lims is None:
        lims = (u.get("utilization") or {}).get("limits") if isinstance(u.get("utilization"), dict) else None
    return lims


def fable_window(lims):
    """The weekly window scoped to Fable in a `limits[]` list, or None. It carries `percent` and
    `resets_at` like the others (probed 2026-10-07); the desktop widget draws it."""
    for x in lims if isinstance(lims, list) else []:
        model = ((x or {}).get("scope") or {}).get("model") or {}
        if (x or {}).get("kind") == "weekly_scoped" and str(model.get("display_name") or "").lower() == "fable":
            return x
    return None


def parse_usage(u):
    rl = u.get("rate_limits") or {}
    out = {"plan": u.get("subscription_type"), "locked": [], "fable": fable_of(u),
           "kinds": [x.get("kind") for x in (rl.get("limits") or []) if isinstance(x, dict)]}
    for w in WINDOWS:
        x = rl.get(w) or {}
        out[w] = x.get("utilization")
        out[w + "_resets"] = epoch(x.get("resets_at"))
        if x.get("locked_reason") or (x.get("utilization") or 0) >= 100:
            out["locked"].append(w)
    # Fable's own week, reported only: a full one parks Fable, not the login, so it stays out of
    # `locked` and out of pick's judgement.
    fw = fable_window(limits_list(u)) or {}
    out["fable_week"] = fw.get("percent")
    out["fable_week_resets"] = epoch(fw.get("resets_at"))
    return out


def cache_path(a):
    return a["home"] / "usage-probe.json"


def rolled(lim, t=None):
    """A cached reading with each window whose reset time has passed read as 0 %: nothing probes
    on an unattended night, so a 5h 90 % from the evening would otherwise block the row until
    something else happened to probe it (review of the autofinish pool, 2026-10-08)."""
    if not lim:
        return lim
    t = now() if t is None else t
    out = dict(lim)
    # Fable's own week rolls too, though it is no window of WINDOWS: that tuple is also pick's
    # locked set and `exhausted --window`'s choices, and a full Fable week parks only Fable.
    for w in WINDOWS + ("fable_week",):
        r = out.get(w + "_resets")
        if r and r <= t and out.get(w) is not None:
            out[w] = 0
            out[w + "_resets"] = None
            out["locked"] = [x for x in (out.get("locked") or []) if x != w]
    return out


def limits(a, cached=False):
    """→ (limits dict or None, sampled epoch or None). Probes only when the cache is older than
    CACHE_S and --cached was not asked for. A cached window past its reset reads 0 (rolled)."""
    if not a["home"].is_dir():
        return None, None
    try:
        c = json.loads(cache_path(a).read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        c = None
    if c and (cached or now() - c.get("at", 0) < CACHE_S):
        return rolled(c.get("limits")), c.get("at")
    if cached:
        return None, None
    lim = probe(a)
    if lim is None:  # a stale reading beats a guess (code review 2026-10-03)
        return (rolled(c.get("limits")), c.get("at")) if c else (None, None)
    try:
        # Written aside and renamed: the widget probes every 10 min while the tier check reads
        # --cached at every prompt, and a half-written file reads as "no probe yet".
        tmp = cache_path(a).with_name(cache_path(a).name + f".{os.getpid()}.tmp")
        tmp.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"at": now(), "limits": lim}))
        os.replace(tmp, cache_path(a))
    except OSError:
        try:
            tmp.unlink()
        except OSError:
            pass
    for w in lim["locked"]:  # R11: the probe is the second writer of an exhaustion
        if a["provider"] == "codex" and (lim.get(w) or 0) < 100:
            continue  # a lock the Codex server reports, not a full window: judge refuses it; no week-long cooldown (review 2026-10-17)
        record_exhaustion(a["home"], w, lim.get(w + "_resets"), lim.get(w), "probe")
    return lim, now()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- exhaustion

def exhaustion_log(home):
    return Path(home) / "exhaustion.jsonl"


def read_exhaustion(home):
    out = []
    try:
        for line in exhaustion_log(home).read_text().splitlines():
            try:
                out.append(json.loads(line))
            except ValueError:
                pass
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


def account_id(home):
    """The login's account id prefix (usage-report names logins by it, never by e-mail)."""
    p = HOME / ".claude.json" if Path(home) == DEFAULT_DIR.absolute() else Path(home) / ".claude.json"
    try:
        return ((json.loads(p.read_text()).get("oauthAccount") or {}).get("accountUuid") or "")[:8] or None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def record_exhaustion(home, window, resets_at, used, session):
    """Append once per window and reset, whoever writes it. The probe's resets_at carries
    microseconds that differ per fetch and the status line's is whole seconds (review
    2026-10-03: three probes of one lock wrote three records, 30 points of margin), so the reset
    is rounded to the minute and anything within SAME_RESET of a recorded one is that one."""
    resets_at = epoch(resets_at)
    resets_at = round(resets_at / 60) * 60 if resets_at else resets_at
    for r in read_exhaustion(home):
        if r.get("window") == window and abs((r.get("resets_at") or 0) - (resets_at or 0)) < SAME_RESET:
            return False
    rec = {"at": now(), "window": window, "used": used, "resets_at": resets_at,
           "session": session, "account": account_id(home)}
    Path(home).mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with exhaustion_log(home).open("a") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(rec) + "\n")
    return True


def cooldown(a, t):
    """The latest reset among windows still locked, or None."""
    ends = [r["resets_at"] for r in read_exhaustion(a["home"]) if (r.get("resets_at") or 0) > t]
    return max(ends) if ends else None


def margin(a, t):
    n = sum(1 for r in read_exhaustion(a["home"]) if t - (r.get("at") or 0) < MARGIN_DAYS * 86400)
    return min(MARGIN * n, MARGIN_CAP)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- pick

def when(t):
    # a weekday names any reset within a week, a week's own included (tier_check prints it so); a
    # month's (Cursor's, via Orca) gets its date
    if not t:
        return "?"
    return dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(t).strftime("%a %H:%M" if t - now() < 7 * 86400 else "%b %-d %H:%M")


def ceiling(a, lim, t):
    """The week ceiling now, and why. `reserve.day` (D2: Saturday) is a local weekday;
    `reserve.last_hours` is the alternative R3 asked about — the hours before the week resets."""
    rules = a["rules"]
    res = rules.get("reserve") or {}
    if res.get("day") and dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(t).strftime("%A").lower() == res["day"].lower():
        return res.get("week_max", 100), f"{res['day']} reserve"
    end = lim.get("seven_day_resets") if lim else None
    if res.get("last_hours") and end and 0 <= end - t <= res["last_hours"] * 3600:
        return res.get("week_max", 100), f"last {res['last_hours']} h of the week"
    return rules.get("week_max", 100), "week ceiling"


def judge(a, lim, t):
    """→ (eligible, reason) for one row with known limits."""
    if a.get("same_as"):
        other, email = a["same_as"]
        return False, f"same login as {other} ({email}), so its limits are {other}'s — sign it in as its own account: {relogin_line(a)}"
    cd = cooldown(a, t)
    if cd:
        return False, f"cooling down until {when(cd)} (a window locked)"
    if lim is None:
        return False, "limits unknown (no probe answered, none cached)"
    week_max, why = ceiling(a, lim, t)
    m = margin(a, t)
    win_max = a["rules"].get("window_max", 100) - m
    wk, fh = lim.get("seven_day"), lim.get("five_hour")
    if a["provider"] == "codex" and lim.get("locked"):
        # the server said the limit is reached; with no reset time no cooldown was written to stop it
        return False, f"locked by Codex ({', '.join(lim['locked'])}); resets {when(lim.get('seven_day_resets') or lim.get('five_hour_resets'))}"
    if a["provider"] == "codex" and (wk is None) != (fh is None):
        # a window the plan does not have is no limit (prolite: the week alone)
        if fh is None:
            return (wk < week_max, f"week {wk}% {'<' if wk < week_max else '≥'} {week_max}% ({why}); no 5-hour window")
        wk = 0
    if wk is None or fh is None:
        return False, "limits unknown (no window figures)"
    if wk >= week_max:
        return False, f"week {wk}% ≥ {week_max}% ({why}); resets {when(lim.get('seven_day_resets'))}"
    if fh > win_max:
        extra = f", {m} points of margin after an exhaustion" if m else ""
        return False, f"5h {fh}% > {win_max}%{extra}; resets {when(lim.get('five_hour_resets'))}"
    return True, f"week {wk}% < {week_max}% ({why}), 5h {fh}% ≤ {win_max}%"


def provider_for(reg, kind, provider):
    if provider:
        return provider
    for p, kinds in (reg.get("capabilities") or {}).items():
        if kind and kind in (kinds or []):
            return p
    return "claude"


def pick(reg, kind=None, provider=None, bound=False, cached=False, repo=None):
    """→ (row, reason, skipped[(label, why)]). Raises Short(skipped) when none may run.
    repo: the path the step runs in; a row whose deny list names its repo is skipped unprobed."""
    t = now()
    prov = provider_for(reg, kind, provider)
    rows = [a for a in reg["accounts"] if a["enabled"] and a["provider"] == prov]
    if bound:
        rows = [a for a in rows if a.get("primary")]
    if not rows:
        raise Short([(prov, f"no enabled {prov} account{' marked primary' if bound else ''} in {REGISTRY}")])
    skipped = []
    own = scope_login_of(repo)
    if own and not own[1]:
        raise Short([("registry", f"{own[0]}: no run picks a seat until it does")])
    if own and not any(a["label"] == own[1] for a in rows):
        raise Short([(own[1], f"scope {own[0]} runs on {own[1]}, which is no enabled {prov} account in {REGISTRY}")])
    for a in rows:
        if own and a["label"] != own[1]:
            skipped.append((a["label"], f"scope {own[0]} runs on {own[1]}"))
            continue
        d = denied(a, repo)
        if d:
            skipped.append((a["label"], off_reason(a, d)))
            continue
        if not a["home"].is_dir():  # one registry for both Macs; the always-on Mac has the primary only
            skipped.append((a["label"], "its login is not in Orca here (no orca-sync has found it)" if via_orca(a)
                            else f"not set up on this machine ({a['home']} missing)"))
            continue
        if a.get("same_as") or cooldown(a, t):  # no probe for a row that cannot run anyway
            skipped.append((a["label"], judge(a, None, t)[1]))
            continue
        lim, _ = limits(a, cached=cached)
        ok, why = judge(a, lim, t)
        if ok:
            return a, why, skipped
        if a.get("primary") and (lim is None or why.startswith("limits unknown")):
            # an unreadable primary (no reading, or one with no window figures; code review 2026-10-17)
            # runs the step rather than nothing once every row before it was tried: the response shape is marked experimental,
            # and a blocked start costs more than a guess (review 2026-10-03). A row after the
            # primary (the Pro fallback, the owner's last resort since 2026-10-16) is for a primary with no room,
            # never one whose room is unknown.
            return a, "limits unknown — the primary runs it rather than nothing", skipped
        if own_tenant(reg, a, repo) and (lim is None or why.startswith("limits unknown")):
            # every other row is refused a tenant's repo, so waiting on an unreadable tenant login waits forever
            return a, "limits unknown — the tenant's own login runs it rather than nothing", skipped
        skipped.append((a["label"], why))
    raise Short(skipped)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- check / add

def auth_status(a):
    if a["provider"] != "claude":
        return None
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([claude_bin(), "auth", "status", "--json"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=30, env=start_env(a))
        return json.loads(r.stdout)
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None


def link_problems(a):
    if is_default(a) or a["provider"] != "claude":
        return []
    bad = []
    for n in LINKS:
        p, target = a["home"] / n, link_target(a, n)
        if not p.is_symlink():
            bad.append(f"{n} is {'a real ' + ('directory' if p.is_dir() else 'file') if p.exists() else 'missing'}")
        elif os.readlink(p) != str(target):
            bad.append(f"{n} → {os.readlink(p)}, not {target}")
        elif n == "CLAUDE.md" and not target.exists():   # a session there would load no me/ file at all
            bad.append(f"{n} → {target}, which does not exist")
    ex = home_memory_excluded(a, a["home"], fix=False)
    if ex:
        bad.append(f"settings.json does not exclude {DEFAULT_DIR / 'CLAUDE.md'} (claudeMdExcludes)")
    return bad


def settings_line(a):
    if a["provider"] != "claude":
        return None
    r = subprocess.run([str(BIN / "claude-settings"), "check"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                       env={**start_env(a), "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": str(a["home"])})
    return (r.stdout.strip().splitlines() or [r.stderr.strip()])[0]


def guard_state(a):
    """`wired` / `missing` for a secondary Claude dir: hooks/account_guard.py (E4) on its
    UserPromptSubmit; None for the default dir and Codex rows (nothing to guard)."""
    if is_default(a) or a["provider"] != "claude":
        return None
    try:
        hooks = (json.loads((a["home"] / "settings.json").read_text()).get("hooks") or {}).get("UserPromptSubmit") or []
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return "missing"
    wired = any("account_guard.py" in (h.get("command") or "") for g in hooks for h in (g.get("hooks") or []))
    return "wired" if wired else "missing"


def worktree_allow_present():
    """wharf's hooks/worktree_allow.py is installed here (G16: without it, its check is skipped)."""
    try:
        return bool(core().tool("worktree_allow.py"))
    except ImportError:
        return False


def worktree_hook(a):
    """True when hooks/worktree_allow.py answers PermissionRequest for both worktree tools in this
    dir (the owner, 2026-10-06: EnterWorktree never asks, on any account)."""
    try:
        groups = (json.loads((a["home"] / "settings.json").read_text()).get("hooks") or {}).get("PermissionRequest") or []
        return any(isinstance(g, dict) and set((g.get("matcher") or "").split("|")) >= {"EnterWorktree", "ExitWorktree"}
                   and any(isinstance(h, dict) and "worktree_allow.py" in (h.get("command") or "")
                           for h in (g.get("hooks") or []))
                   for g in groups)
    except Exception:
        return False


def cmd_check(reg):
    if not reg["accounts"]:
        print(f"account: no registry at {REGISTRY}")
        return 0
    bad = 0
    t = now()
    for a in reg["accounts"]:
        if not a["enabled"]:
            print(f"  --  {a['label']} ({a['provider']}): disabled")
            continue
        if not a["home"].is_dir():
            print(f"  --  {a['label']}: " + ("its login is not in Orca here — " + orca_login_line(a) if via_orca(a)
                                             else "not on this machine"))
            continue
        notes = []
        if a["provider"] == "codex":  # no Claude dir: trust, settings and the guard are not its; the reading is
            lim, at = limits(a, cached=True)
            notes.append(f"Codex, {(lim or {}).get('plan') or 'plan ?'}; " + (f"probe {int((t - at) / 60)} min old" if at else "never probed"))
            print(f"  {'ok' if at else '⚠ '}  {a['label']}: " + "; ".join(notes))
            continue
        st = auth_status(a)
        if not st or not st.get("loggedIn"):
            bad += 1
            cmd = f"{BIN / 'account'} add {a['label']}"
            notes.append("NOT logged in — " + orca_login_line(a) if via_orca(a) else
                         f"NOT logged in — run {cmd}" + ("" if is_default(a) else " (it opens the /login)"))
        else:
            notes.append(f"{st.get('email') or st.get('emailAddress') or '?'} ({st.get('subscriptionType') or '?'}"
                         f"{' / ' + seat_of(a) if seat_of(a) else ''})")
        lp = link_problems(a)
        if lp:
            bad += 1
            notes.append("links: " + "; ".join(lp))
        if not is_default(a):
            tr = subprocess.run(trust_argv(a, "--check"), capture_output=True, text=True,
                                env={**os.environ, "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": str(a["home"])})
            if tr.returncode:
                notes.append(f"{tr.stdout.strip().splitlines()[-1].strip() if tr.stdout.strip() else 'trust unknown'}"
                             f" — CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']} {BIN / 'trust-projects'}")
        sl = settings_line(a)
        if sl and not sl.endswith(", 0 missing, 0 undeclared"):
            notes.append(sl)
        g = guard_state(a)
        if g:
            notes.append(f"guard: {g}")
        if a.get("same_as"):
            bad += 1
            notes.append(judge(a, None, t)[1])
        lim, at = limits(a, cached=True)
        notes.append(f"probe {int((t - at) / 60)} min old" if at else "never probed")
        cd = cooldown(a, t)
        if cd:
            notes.append(f"cooling down until {when(cd)}")
        mark = "ok" if not lp and not a.get("same_as") and (st or {}).get("loggedIn") else "⚠ "
        print(f"  {mark}  {a['label']}{' (primary)' if a.get('primary') else ''}: " + "; ".join(notes))
    return 1 if bad else 0


def open_in_terminal(argv, env):
    """A new Terminal window running argv with env (wharf's fork_claude_session, with the env
    prefix); without wharf, the command to type, printed (G16)."""
    try:
        fcs = core().tool("fork_claude_session.py")
    except ImportError:
        fcs = None
    if not fcs:
        print("  (wharf's fork_claude_session.py not installed: no window opened — run it yourself:) "
              + " ".join(f"{k}={shlex.quote(v)}" for k, v in env.items()) + " " + shlex.join(argv))
        return
    sys.path.insert(0, str(fcs.parent))
    from fork_claude_session import open_in_terminal as oit
    oit(HOME, argv, env=env)


def cmd_add(reg, label, login, say_login=True):
    a = row(reg, label)
    if a["provider"] != "claude":
        sys.exit(f"account: adding a {a['provider']} account is not built — sign its home in yourself "
                 f"(`CODEX_HOME={shlex.quote(str(a['home']))} codex login`), then `account limits {label}`; orca-sync makes rows of its own, never fills this one")
    if is_default(a):
        print(f"{label} is the default dir {DEFAULT_DIR}; nothing to make — /login there if it is logged out")
        return 0
    if via_orca(a):
        # Orca builds and wires that dir and holds its login: never a mkdir, link set or login of ours
        print(f"account: {label}'s login is Orca's — {orca_login_line(a)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    home = a["home"]
    home.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    memory_bad = False
    for n in LINKS:
        p, target = home / n, link_target(a, n)
        if n == "CLAUDE.md":   # an employer row's points at the work file, an older link re-pointed
            line = point_memory(a, home)
            print(line or f"  ok      {p}")
            ex = home_memory_excluded(a, home)
            if ex:
                print(ex)
            memory_bad = bool((line and "⚠" in line) or (ex and "⚠" in ex))   # setup did not finish
            continue
        if p.is_symlink() or p.exists():
            print(f"  {'ok    ' if p.is_symlink() and os.readlink(p) == str(target) else '⚠ left'}  {p}")
            continue
        p.symlink_to(target)
        print(f"  linked  {p} → {target}")
    # trust-projects needs a .claude.json to write into, and the login has not made one yet; an
    # empty one is what Claude Code starts from (code review 2026-10-03: without it, trust failed
    # and nothing ran it again)
    if not (home / ".claude.json").exists():
        (home / ".claude.json").write_text("{}\n")
    env = {**os.environ, "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": str(home)}
    for argv in ([bootstrap_cmd(), "--hooks"], trust_argv(a)):
        if argv[0] == BOOTSTRAP_PLACEHOLDER:   # no overlay: nothing wires the hooks but the plugin (README)
            print(f"  skip    the overlay's bootstrap.sh --hooks for {home}: no overlay here")
            continue
        r = subprocess.run(argv, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True)
        print(f"  {'ran   ' if r.returncode == 0 else '⚠ fail'}  {Path(argv[0]).name} {' '.join(argv[1:])} for {home}"
              + ("" if r.returncode == 0 else f": {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:200]}"))
    key = hashlib.sha256(str(home).encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
    if login:
        open_in_terminal([claude_bin(), "auth", "login"], {"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": str(home)})
        print(f"  → a Terminal window runs `claude auth login` for {home} — sign in there as {label}"
              f" (keychain entry: Claude Code-credentials-{key})")
    elif say_login:  # setup prints its own line, in the ssh -t form on a peer
        print(f"  login: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={shlex.quote(str(home))} claude auth login")
    return 1 if memory_bad else 0


def cmd_setup(reg, record=True, peer=None):
    """Build this Mac from the logins file: a dir for every Claude row the file lists for this Mac,
    the login line for every dir not signed in as the file says, and the e-mail and plan of a login
    the file lacks written there. Idempotent: a second run prints `ok` per row. → exit 1 when a
    login is owed."""
    path, logins = load_logins()
    if path is None:
        print("account: no logins file on this Mac (the config dir's lighthouse/logins.json; Drive not mounted?) — nothing to build from")
        return 1
    if not logins:
        print(f"account: no logins file at {path}")
        return 1
    mac, owed, changed = this_mac(), 0, False
    for a in reg["accounts"]:
        if a["provider"] != "claude":
            continue
        e = logins.get(a["label"])
        if e is None:
            print(f"  --  {a['label']}: not in the logins file {path}")
            continue
        macs = e.get("macs") or []
        if mac not in macs:
            print(f"  --  {a['label']}: not listed for {mac}" + (f" ({e['note']})" if e.get("note") else ""))
            continue
        if not a["home"].is_dir() and via_orca(a):
            owed += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {a['label']}: its login is not in Orca on {mac} — {orca_login_line(a)}")
            continue
        if not a["home"].is_dir():
            print(f"  add {a['label']}: {a['home']} missing on {mac}")
            cmd_add(reg, a["label"], False, say_login=False)
        _, email = login_of(a)
        want = e.get("email")
        if not email:
            owed += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {a['label']}: not signed in on {mac} — sign in as {want or 'its own account'}:\n      {login_line(a, peer)}")
        elif not want:
            if record:
                st = auth_status(a) or {}
                e.update({"email": email, "plan": st.get("subscriptionType"), "org_type": seat_of(a)})
                changed = True
                print(f"  rec {a['label']}: {email} ({e['plan'] or '?'}) recorded in {path}")
            else:
                print(f"  ok  {a['label']}: {email} (the laptop records it in the logins file)")
        elif email.lower() != want.lower():
            owed += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {a['label']}: signed in as {email} on {mac}, the logins file says {want}:\n      {login_line(a, peer)}")
        else:
            print(f"  ok  {a['label']}: {email}" + (f" ({e['plan']})" if e.get("plan") else ""))
    if changed:
        save_logins(path, logins)
    return 1 if owed else 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- Orca's logins, ships

def orca_bin():
    return os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_ORCA") or shutil.which("orca") or (ORCA_APP_CLI if os.path.exists(ORCA_APP_CLI) else None)


def orca_claude_accounts():
    """→ Orca's managed Claude accounts as `orca account list --json` prints them, or exits."""
    accts = (orca_list().get("claude") or {}).get("accounts") or []
    return [x for x in accts if isinstance(x, dict)]


def orca_read():
    """→ (the `result` of `orca account list --json`, None), or (None, why there is none).
    ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE is cleared: VS Code's terminals set it and the app's CLI then runs as
    bare node (lessons/claude-code.md)."""
    o = orca_bin()
    if not o:
        return None, "no orca CLI here (install the Orca app)"
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k != "ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE"}
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([o, "account", "list", "--json"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, env=env)
        d = json.loads(r.stdout)
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        return None, f"`orca account list` gave nothing readable ({e}) — is the app running?"
    if not isinstance(d, dict) or not d.get("ok"):
        return None, f"`orca account list` refused: {json.dumps(d.get('error') if isinstance(d, dict) else d)[:300]}"
    return d.get("result") or {}, None


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=1)  # orca-sync's two halves read one snapshot, one Electron start (review 2026-10-16)
def orca_list():
    """→ the `result` of `orca account list --json` (every agent's accounts), or exits."""
    res, err = orca_read()
    if err:
        sys.exit(f"account: {err}")
    return res


# Claude and Codex are probed here, login by login, and Orca's figure for each is one of those
# logins' own; every other agent in Orca's `rateLimits` is reported from Orca alone (`limits --orca`).
ORCA_PROBED = ("claude", "codex")
ORCA_WINDOWS = {"session": "five_hour", "weekly": "seven_day"}   # Orca's window names → WINDOWS


def parse_orca(x):
    """One agent's `rateLimits` entry in Orca's list → the limits dict probe() gives, plus `buckets`,
    the agent's own quota groups (Antigravity's Gemini and Claude/GPT models, Cursor's), and `windows`,
    the ones ORCA_WINDOWS does not name (a month), each {name, percent, resets, window_s}. A full
    bucket parks those models, not the login, so like Fable's week it stays out of `locked`; an
    agent with buckets has its week as its fuller bucket (Antigravity's), so a full week locks
    nothing either (review 2026-10-21). Orca's times are milliseconds."""
    ms = lambda v: v / 1000 if isinstance(v, (int, float)) else None
    win = lambda v: isinstance(v, dict) and isinstance(v.get("usedPercent"), (int, float))
    bucket = lambda name, v: {"name": name, "percent": v["usedPercent"], "resets": ms(v.get("resetsAt")),
                              "window_s": v["windowMinutes"] * 60 if isinstance(v.get("windowMinutes"), (int, float)) else None}
    groups = [bucket(str(b.get("name") or "?"), b) for b in x.get("buckets") or [] if win(b)]
    out = {"plan": x.get("planType"), "locked": [], "fable": None, "kinds": [],
           "fable_week": None, "fable_week_resets": None, "buckets": groups, "windows": []}
    for w in WINDOWS:
        out[w], out[w + "_resets"] = None, None
    for k, v in x.items():
        if not win(v):
            continue
        if k in ORCA_WINDOWS:
            w = ORCA_WINDOWS[k]
            out[w], out[w + "_resets"] = v["usedPercent"], ms(v.get("resetsAt"))
            out["kinds"].append(w)
            if v["usedPercent"] >= 100 and not (groups and w == "seven_day"):
                out["locked"].append(w)
        else:
            out["windows"].append(bucket({"monthly": "month"}.get(k, k), v))
    # a window of the buckets' own span is their summary (Cursor's month, as Antigravity's week)
    spans = {b["window_s"] for b in groups}
    out["windows"] = [w for w in out["windows"] if w["window_s"] not in spans]
    return out


def agent_label(p, taken):
    """The label of Orca's agent `p`: its own name, or `orca-<name>` made unique when a row (the
    registry's, an Orca login's) or an earlier agent has it — never a shadow of either."""
    return p if p not in taken else orca_label({"email": p}, taken)


def orca_row(p, label, lim, at, why):
    """A `limits --orca` row, in limits_json's shape: report only, so never eligible. A bucket or
    window past its reset reads 0, as rolled() reads the others (an Orca down for a week)."""
    lim = rolled(lim)
    if lim:
        t = now()
        lim = dict(lim, **{k: [dict(b, percent=0, resets=None) if b.get("resets") and b["resets"] <= t else b
                               for b in lim.get(k) or [] if isinstance(b, dict)] for k in ("buckets", "windows")})
    return {"label": label, "provider": p, "primary": False, "enabled": True, "here": True, "home": None,
            "env": {}, "limits": lim, "sampled": at, "eligible": False, "why": why,
            "denied": None, "same_as": None, "via": "orca", "report": True}


def orca_reported(taken=(), cached=False):
    """→ the rows `limits --all --orca` adds: one per agent Orca reads limits for, bar ORCA_PROBED
    (the header), kept in ORCA_SEEN under Orca's name for it. An agent signed out, or never read, that
    Orca calls `unavailable` is none; any other that answers without figures keeps its last reading and says
    Orca's error. Every last reading in ORCA_SEEN, with its own age, when Orca is not asked
    (--cached), cannot be read (the app not running), lists no agent (not ready yet: rebuilding from
    that would wipe them all) or answers in a shape this does not read — never a traceback, whose
    stderr would fail the widget's whole reply. `orca off NAME` hides one."""
    seen = {k: e for k, e in read_json(ORCA_SEEN, {}).items() if isinstance(e, dict)}
    off = {k for k, e in seen.items() if e.get("enabled") is False}
    used = set(taken)

    def label(p):
        lab = agent_label(p, used)
        used.add(lab)
        return lab

    def last(why):
        return [orca_row(p, label(p), e.get("limits"), e.get("at"), why)
                for p, e in seen.items() if p not in off and e.get("limits")]

    if cached:
        return last("Orca's last reading (--cached asks it nothing)")
    res, err = orca_read()
    if err:
        return last(f"Orca unreadable ({err}); its last reading")
    try:
        rl = res.get("rateLimits") if isinstance(res, dict) else None
        agents = [x for x in (rl.values() if isinstance(rl, dict) else [])
                  if isinstance(x, dict) and isinstance(x.get("provider"), str) and x["provider"] not in ORCA_PROBED]
        if not agents:
            return last("Orca listed no agent (not ready yet); its last reading")
        keep, rows = {p: {"enabled": False} for p in off}, []
        for x in agents:
            p = x["provider"]
            prev = seen.get(p, {})
            # `unavailable` is Orca not asking: signed out (missing-credentials), but also a CLI too
            # old to answer /usage or a provider switched off (review 2026-10-21, Orca's source). A
            # sign-out, or an agent never read, is no card; any other keeps its last reading, said so.
            if x.get("status") == "unavailable" and (not prev.get("limits")
                                                     or (x.get("usageMetadata") or {}).get("failureKind") == "missing-credentials"):
                continue
            if x.get("status") == "ok":
                u = x.get("updatedAt")
                lim, why = parse_orca(x), "reported by Orca; report only, never walked"
                at = u / 1000 if isinstance(u, (int, float)) and u > 0 else None
            else:
                lim, at = prev.get("limits"), prev.get("at")
                why = f"Orca: {x.get('error') or x.get('status')}" + ("; its last reading" if lim else "")
            keep[p] = dict(keep.get(p, {}), at=at, limits=lim)
            if p not in off:
                rows.append(orca_row(p, label(p), lim, at, why))
    except Exception as e:  # an Orca whose answer changed shape: its last readings, said so
        used = set(taken)
        return last(f"Orca's answer unreadable ({type(e).__name__}); its last reading")
    # An `orca on|off` written while Orca answered wins over the switch read before (no lock: the
    # widget's 10-minute refresh is the only other writer, and its next run honours the switch).
    for p, e in read_json(ORCA_SEEN, {}).items():
        if isinstance(e, dict) and "enabled" in e:
            keep[p] = dict(keep.get(p, {}), enabled=e["enabled"])
    if keep != seen:
        try:
            write_json(ORCA_SEEN, keep)
        except OSError:
            pass  # a reading not kept costs only the stale fallback
    return rows


def orca_codex_sync(reg, off=False):
    """Every Codex login Orca uses → a row (the owner, 2026-10-16: the widget shows "all accounts
    registered in Orca, including Codex/ChatGPT"): its system default, .codex in the home folder, and each managed
    account, whose home is ORCA_DATA/codex-accounts/<id>/home (Orca's source, app.asar, read
    2026-10-16; no managed Codex account existed to see one). Recorded in ORCA_FILE with
    `"provider": "codex"` only once Codex itself, asked in that home (account/read), is signed in
    as the e-mail Orca names; an entry Orca no longer lists goes. Labels are `codex-<local part>`,
    kept across syncs by Orca's id. --off and `orca default off` leave Codex logins alone (the walk
never takes one; `off` stays in the signature for the caller), `orca off LABEL` still hides one.
    → 1 if a login Orca names could not be recorded."""
    res = orca_list()
    cx = res.get("codex") or {}
    found = []  # (orca id, e-mail, home)
    sd = cx.get("systemDefault") or {}
    if sd.get("hasAuth") and sd.get("email"):
        # the home folder's .codex, not the caller's $CODEX_HOME: Orca's systemDefault is read in Orca's
        # own process (app.asar: process.env.CODEX_HOME there, else that .codex), and a sync run from a Codex worker's
        # shell would look in its home instead (review 2026-10-17)
        found.append(("system-default", sd["email"], HOME / ".codex"))
    for x in cx.get("accounts") or []:
        if isinstance(x, dict) and x.get("id"):
            found.append((str(x["id"]), x.get("email") or "", ORCA_DATA / "codex-accounts" / str(x["id"]) / "home"))
    synced = read_json(ORCA_FILE, {})
    mine = {k: v for k, v in synced.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and v.get("provider") == "codex"}
    by_id = {v.get("orca_id"): k for k, v in mine.items()}
    # gone: only an entry a Codex sync recorded (it has Orca's id), and only when Orca's list has a
    # Codex section to judge by — a list without one, or an entry written by hand, says nothing; and
    # an empty section is Orca not ready, never every login gone, as for Claude (review 2026-10-16)
    if "codex" in res and not found and mine:
        print("  --      Orca lists no Codex login right now — every Codex entry kept as it was")
    gone = [k for k, v in mine.items() if v.get("orca_id") and v["orca_id"] not in {f[0] for f in found}] if "codex" in res and found else []
    for label in gone:
        synced.pop(label)
        write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
        print(f"  gone    {label}: no longer a Codex login in Orca — out of the rows")

    def drop(label, why):  # an entry that no longer names the login it was recorded for
        if label and label in synced:
            synced.pop(label)
            write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
            print(f"  gone    {label}: {why} — out of the rows")
    bad = 0
    named = {r["label"] for r in reg["accounts"] if not r.get("implicit")}  # a registry row's label would shadow it
    for oid, email, home in found:
        label = by_id.get(oid)
        if label and (synced[label].get("email") or "").lower() != email.lower():
            # a home signed in to another account is another login: a new label, not the old one's
            # switch, exhaustion and cooldown history
            drop(label, f"Orca's {oid} is {email or 'nobody'} now, not {synced[label].get('email')}")
            label = None
        other = next((k for k, v in synced.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and v.get("provider") == "codex"
                      and v.get("orca_id") != oid and (v.get("email") or "").lower() == email.lower()), None)
        if other:  # Orca's system default and a managed account on one e-mail are one quota, never two rows
            print(f"  --      Orca's Codex {email} ({oid}) is {other}'s login already — not a row of its own")
            drop(label, f"its login is {other}'s")
            continue
        if not label:
            label = orca_label({"email": email, "id": oid}, set(synced) | named, "codex-")
        if not home.is_dir():
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: Orca names Codex login {email or oid}, but {home} is no dir — nothing recorded")
            continue
        ans = codex_rpc(home, [("account/read", {})]).get(2)
        if not isinstance(ans, dict) or "error" in ans:
            # no answer (no codex binary, a timeout, an error reply) says nothing about the sign-in:
            # the entry and its switch stay, as for an empty Orca list (code review 2026-10-17)
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: Codex in {home} gave no answer to account/read — entry kept as it was")
            continue
        who = ans.get("account") or {}
        seen = who.get("email") or ""
        if not email or seen.lower() != email.lower():
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: Codex in {home} is signed in as {seen or 'nobody'}, Orca says {email or '?'} — nothing recorded")
            drop(label if label in mine else None, "its home is not the login Orca names")
            continue
        had = synced.get(label) or {}
        entry = {"provider": "codex", "home": str(home), "orca_id": oid, "email": email}
        if isinstance(had.get("enabled"), bool):
            entry["enabled"] = had["enabled"]   # a switch made earlier stays
        # --off and `orca default off` are about the walk, which never takes a Codex row; applied
        # here they would only hide the login from the widget (review 2026-10-17). `orca off` still can.
        synced[label] = entry
        write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
        print(f"  {'ok ' if had.get('home') == str(home) else 'new'} {label}: {home} — Codex, signed in as {seen} "
              f"({who.get('planType') or '?'})" + ("" if entry.get("enabled") is not False else f"; off — `account orca on {label}`"))
    return 1 if bad else 0


def signed_in_as(d):
    """The e-mail a config dir's .claude.json is signed in as (login_of's read), or None."""
    try:
        o = json.loads((Path(d) / ".claude.json").read_text()).get("oauthAccount") or {}
        return o.get("emailAddress") if isinstance(o, dict) else None
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None


def orca_home_of(x):
    """→ (dir or None, why). Orca's list names no dir in 1.4.225, so the dir is the one under
    ORCA_DATA (four levels at most) whose .claude.json is signed in as the entry's e-mail —
    one with the entry's id in its path first. Two or none: None, said, never guessed."""
    email = (x.get("email") or "").lower()
    if not email:
        return None, f"Orca's entry {x.get('id')} carries no e-mail to find its dir by"
    found = []
    base = str(ORCA_DATA)
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(base):
        if root[len(base):].count(os.sep) >= 4:
            dirs[:] = []
        dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if d not in ("Cache", "Code Cache", "GPUCache", "logs", "Crashpad", "projects", "sessions")]
        if ".claude.json" in files and (signed_in_as(root) or "").lower() == email:
            found.append(root)
    by_id = [f for f in found if x.get("id") and str(x["id"]) in f]
    pick = by_id if len(by_id) == 1 else found
    if len(pick) == 1:
        return pick[0], None
    return None, (f"{len(found)} dirs under {ORCA_DATA} are signed in as {email}" if found
                  else f"no dir under {ORCA_DATA} is signed in as {email} yet")


def orca_label(x, taken, prefix="orca-"):
    """A label for an Orca login no row names: `orca-<the e-mail's local part>`, made unique."""
    base = prefix + (re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", (x.get("email") or str(x.get("id")))[:40].split("@")[0].lower()).strip("-") or "account")
    label, n = base, 2
    while label in taken:
        label, n = f"{base}-{n}", n + 1
    return label


def cmd_orca_sync(reg, off=False):
    """Every Claude login in Orca → a row the walk can pick (the owner, 2026-10-16: "auto-picker gets all
    Orca accounts enabled by default, but there's a way to override during set up, or change it later
    at any point"). Registry rows of Orca's claim first: the id an earlier sync recorded, else the
    logins file's e-mail for the label, else — only when that file has none — the one account nobody
    claimed; a registry row's claim takes an account back from an implicit row that held it. Every
    account left over becomes a row of its own (orca_label; hooks/common.py account_rows), in the walk —
    or recorded off with --off or while `orca default off` stands — unless its e-mail is another row's
    login, which that row decides. An implicit login gone from Orca leaves the walk. Its dir is found by
    e-mail (orca_home_of), Claude Code must read that login there, and only then is it recorded in
    ORCA_FILE. The wiring is Orca's; ours is the two links Orca leaves out (`projects`, `sessions`: one
    transcript store), trust, and — for a login synced for the first time — its VS Code profile.
    → exit 1 if a login stays unsynced, or a dir's CLAUDE.md was left or failed (point_memory)."""
    sys.path.insert(0, core_hooks())
    from common import orca_row
    accts = orca_claude_accounts()
    ids = {x.get("id") for x in accts}
    _, logins = load_logins()
    synced = read_json(ORCA_FILE, {})
    entries = {k: v for k, v in synced.items() if not k.startswith("_") and isinstance(v, dict)}
    implicit = {a["label"] for a in reg["accounts"] if a.get("implicit") and a["provider"] == "claude"}  # a Codex entry is never pruned from Claude's list
    work = []  # (row, the Orca account or None, the logins file's e-mail for the row)
    claimed = set()
    for a in [a for a in reg["accounts"] if via_orca(a) and not a.get("implicit") and a["provider"] == "claude"]:
        had = entries.get(a["label"]) or {}
        want = ((logins.get(a["label"]) or {}).get("email") or "").lower()
        match = [x for x in accts if had.get("orca_id") and x.get("id") == had["orca_id"]]
        match = match or [x for x in accts if want and (x.get("email") or "").lower() == want]
        if not match and not want:
            held = {(entries.get(r) or {}).get("orca_id") for r in implicit}
            free = [x for x in accts if x.get("id") not in claimed and x.get("id") not in held]
            match = free if len(free) == 1 else []
        if match:
            claimed.add(match[0].get("id"))
        work.append((a, match[0] if match else None, want))
    for label in sorted(implicit):  # an implicit login: kept while Orca has it and no registry row took it
        oid = (entries.get(label) or {}).get("orca_id")
        if oid not in ids and not accts:  # an empty list is Orca not ready, never every login gone: keep its switch
            print(f"  --      {label}: Orca lists no Claude account right now — kept as it was")
            continue
        if oid not in ids or oid in claimed:
            synced.pop(label, None)
            write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
            reg["accounts"] = [r for r in reg["accounts"] if r["label"] != label]
            print(f"  gone    {label}: " + ("no longer in Orca — out of the walk" if oid not in ids else "its login is a registry row's now"))
            continue
        a = next(r for r in reg["accounts"] if r["label"] == label)
        work.append((a, next(x for x in accts if x.get("id") == oid), ""))
        claimed.add(oid)
    others = {(e.get("email") or "").lower(): lab for lab, e in logins.items() if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("email")}
    for r in reg["accounts"]:
        if not via_orca(r) and r["provider"] == "claude" and r["home"].is_dir():
            others.setdefault((login_of(r)[1] or "").lower(), r["label"])
    others.pop("", None)
    taken = {a["label"] for a in reg["accounts"]} | set(synced)
    early_bad = 0
    for x in accts:
        if x.get("id") in claimed:
            continue
        owner = others.get((x.get("email") or "").lower())
        if owner:
            print(f"  --      Orca's {x.get('email')} is {owner}'s login — that row decides; not a row of its own")
            # what its sessions load is still that row's call: an employer row's gets the work file
            row_of = next((r for r in reg["accounts"] if r["label"] == owner), None)
            home, _ = orca_home_of(x)
            if row_of is not None and home:
                line = point_memory(row_of, home)
                if line:
                    print(line)
                    early_bad += "⚠" in line
            continue
        label = orca_label(x, taken)
        taken.add(label)
        a = dict(orca_row(label), home=HOME / ".orca-unsynced" / label)
        reg["accounts"].append(a)
        work.append((a, x, ""))
        claimed.add(x.get("id"))
    if not work:
        print("account: Orca holds no Claude login of its own, and no row says \"via\": \"orca\"")
        return 1 if early_bad else 0
    bad = early_bad
    for a, x, want in work:
        label, had = a["label"], entries.get(a["label"]) or {}
        if x is None:
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: no Orca Claude account is it ({len(accts)} in Orca"
                  f"{', none signed in as ' + want if want else ''}) — {orca_login_line(a)}")
            continue
        if want and (x.get("email") or "").lower() != want:
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: Orca's account {x.get('id')} is {x.get('email')}, the logins file says {want} — "
                  f"{orca_login_line(a)}")
            continue
        home, why = orca_home_of(x)
        if not home:
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: {why} — nothing recorded (Orca account {x.get('id')})")
            continue
        a["home"] = Path(home).absolute()
        # before the login read: over ssh the keychain is shut and every Orca login reads logged out
        # (lessons/claude-code.md), and what an employer session loads must not wait on that
        for line in (point_memory(a, a["home"]), home_memory_excluded(a, a["home"])):
            if line:
                print(line)
                bad += "⚠" in line   # a link left or failed, an exclusion not made: the sync did not do its job
        st = auth_status(a) or {}
        if not st.get("loggedIn"):
            bad += 1
            print(f"  ⚠   {label}: Claude Code reads no login in {home} — nothing recorded; {orca_login_line(a)}")
            continue
        for n in ("projects", "sessions"):
            p = a["home"] / n
            if not p.exists() and not p.is_symlink():
                p.symlink_to(DEFAULT_DIR / n)
                print(f"  linked  {p} → {DEFAULT_DIR / n}")
            elif not (p.is_symlink() and os.readlink(p) == str(DEFAULT_DIR / n)):
                print(f"  ⚠ left  {p}: Orca's own — its transcripts stay there")
        r = subprocess.run(trust_argv(a), capture_output=True, text=True,   # re-applied each sync: Orca
                           env={**os.environ, "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": home})   # copies the home .claude.json's projects over
        if r.returncode:
            print(f"  ⚠ fail  trust-projects for {home}: {(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:200]}")
        new = not had
        same = had.get("home") == home and had.get("orca_id") == x.get("id")
        entry = {"home": home, "orca_id": x.get("id"), "email": x.get("email")}
        if isinstance(had.get("enabled"), bool):
            entry["enabled"] = had["enabled"]   # a switch made earlier stays
        elif new and a.get("implicit") and (off or synced.get("_default") == "off"):
            entry["enabled"] = False            # --off or `orca default off`: a new login is recorded off
        synced[label] = entry
        write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
        a["enabled"] = a.get("enabled", True) and entry.get("enabled") is not False
        a.pop("same_as", None)
        mark_same_logins(reg)  # a new login that is another row's is no second account, nor a profile
        print(f"  {'ok ' if same else 'new'} {label}: {home} — Orca account {x.get('id')}, signed in as "
              f"{st.get('email') or st.get('emailAddress') or x.get('email')} ({st.get('subscriptionType') or '?'}); "
              + (f"same login as {a['same_as'][0]} — never picked" if a.get("same_as") else
                 "in the walk" if a["enabled"] else f"off — `account orca on {label}` puts it in the walk"))
        # its VS Code profile (orca-accounts D2), once, when first synced, in a GUI session here — never
        # over ssh, and never again after the owner deletes it (`account vscode LABEL` makes it again); a
        # failure is said, and the login stays synced: exit 1 means a login unsynced or a memory link not made, nothing else
        if (new and a["enabled"] and not a.get("same_as") and not os.environ.get("SSH_CONNECTION")
                and vscode_state()[0] is not None and os.path.exists(CODE_CLI)):
            if cmd_vscode(reg, label):
                print(f"  ⚠       {label}: synced, but its VS Code profile is not set — `account vscode {label}` says why")
    return 1 if bad else 0


def cmd_orca_switch(reg, what, label=None):
    """`orca on|off LABEL`: one Orca login in or out of the walk, on this Mac, at once — no commit
    (the owner: "We'll switch it off later, make it easy"); `on` never overrides a registry row's own
    `enabled: false`. `orca default on|off`: whether a login orca-sync finds from now on starts in the
    walk (the switch made "during set up"; `orca-sync --off` does it for one run); a login already
    synced keeps its place."""
    synced = read_json(ORCA_FILE, {})
    if label is None:
        print("account: orca on|off LABEL, or orca default on|off", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if what == "default":
        if label not in ("on", "off"):
            print("account: orca default takes on or off", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        synced["_default"] = label
        write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
        print(f"Orca logins orca-sync finds from now on start {'in' if label == 'on' else 'out of'} the walk"
              f" (those already synced keep their place)")
        return 0
    e = synced.get(label)
    if not isinstance(e, dict) or label.startswith("_"):
        # An agent `limits --orca` reports (Antigravity, Cursor): no walk to leave, only its card.
        # Kept under Orca's name for it, so the switch follows the agent whatever its card is called;
        # the card's `orca-<name>` label is taken too. One not read yet is looked up in Orca itself.
        seen = read_json(ORCA_SEEN, {})
        res, _ = orca_read() if not any(k in seen for k in (label, label[5:] if label.startswith("orca-") else None)) else (None, None)
        known = set(seen) | {x.get("provider") for x in (((res or {}).get("rateLimits") or {}).values() if isinstance(res, dict) else [])
                             if isinstance(x, dict) and x.get("provider") not in ORCA_PROBED}
        p = label if label in known else label[5:] if label.startswith("orca-") and label[5:] in known else None
        if p:
            seen[p] = dict(seen.get(p) if isinstance(seen.get(p), dict) else {}, enabled=what == "on")
            write_json(ORCA_SEEN, seen)
            print(f"{p}: its card {'shown' if what == 'on' else 'hidden'} in limits --orca (the widget) on this Mac from now on")
            return 0
        print(f"account: {label} is no Orca login synced on this Mac ({ORCA_FILE}; account orca-sync records them)"
              f" and no agent Orca reads limits for", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    e["enabled"] = what == "on"
    write_json(ORCA_FILE, synced)
    parked = what == "on" and any(r["label"] == label and not r.get("implicit") and r.get("enabled") is False
                                  for r in json.loads(REGISTRY.read_text()).get("accounts") or [] if isinstance(r, dict)) \
        if REGISTRY.is_file() else False
    print(f"{label}: " + ("still out: accounts.json parks it (enabled: false)" if parked else
                          "in the walk" if what == "on" else "out of the walk") + " on this Mac from now on")
    return 0


@contextlib.contextmanager
def ships_lock():
    """SHIPS is read-modify-write: concurrent `pick --ship` calls (a fan-out of ships) take turns."""
    SHIPS.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with open(SHIPS.with_name(SHIPS.name + ".lock"), "w") as f:
        fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        try:
            yield
        finally:
            fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)


def load_ships():
    t = now()
    return {k: v for k, v in read_json(SHIPS, {}).items()
            if isinstance(v, dict) and t - float(v.get("at") or 0) < PIN_DAYS * 86400}


def pick_for_ship(reg, key, kind=None, provider=None, cached=False, repo=None, pin=True):
    """pick, but a ship stays on its login (module docstring). → (row, reason, skipped); Short as pick."""
    with ships_lock():
        ships = load_ships()
        pinned = ships.get(key)
        prov = provider_for(reg, kind, provider)
        a = next((r for r in reg["accounts"] if pinned and r["label"] == pinned.get("label")), None)
        moved = None
        if a is not None:
            d = denied(a, repo)
            own = scope_login_of(repo)
            gone = (not a["enabled"] and "disabled") or (a["provider"] != prov and f"not a {prov} account") \
                or (not a["home"].is_dir() and "not here") or (a.get("same_as") and "on another row's login") \
                or (d and f"denied for {d}") or (own and own[1] != a["label"] and f"not scope {own[0]}'s login {own[1]}")
            if not gone:
                t = now()
                lim = None if cooldown(a, t) else limits(a, cached=cached)[0]
                ok, why = judge(a, lim, t)
                if not ok and lim is None and a.get("primary") and not cooldown(a, t):
                    ok, why = True, "limits unknown — the primary runs it rather than nothing"
                elif not ok and (lim is None or why.startswith("limits unknown")) and own_tenant(reg, a, repo) \
                        and not cooldown(a, t):  # as pick reads it: a reading with no window figures too (review 2026-10-21)
                    ok, why = True, "limits unknown — the tenant's own login runs it rather than nothing"
                if ok:
                    if pin:  # a pin lasts PIN_DAYS from its last use, so a long ship keeps it
                        ships[key]["at"] = int(now())
                        write_json(SHIPS, ships)
                    return a, f"ship {key} stays on it: {why}", []
                raise Short([(a["label"], f"ship {key} stays on {a['label']} and waits: {why}")])
            moved = f"{pinned['label']} is {gone}"
        elif pinned:
            moved = f"{pinned.get('label')} is no row any more"
        a, why, skipped = pick(reg, kind, provider, False, cached, repo)
        if pin:
            ships[key] = {"label": a["label"], "at": int(now())}
            write_json(SHIPS, ships)
        return a, f"ship {key} {'moved: ' + moved + '; ' if moved else 'pinned: ' if pin else 'unpinned: '}{why}", skipped


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- doctor (E3)

VSCODE_USER = Path(os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_VSCODE_USER") or HOME / "Library/Application Support/Code/User")
EXPIRY_WARN_S = 3 * 86400


def wrap_peer(cmd, peer, tty=False):
    """A fix line typed on the laptop for the peer: the command over ssh, quoted once."""
    return f"ssh {'-t ' if tty else ''}{peer} {shlex.quote(cmd)}" if peer else cmd


def vscode_state():
    """→ (profiles {name: location}, associations {uri: profile location}) from VS Code's
    storage.json (work-account P8); ({}, {}) when VS Code is not on this Mac."""
    try:
        d = json.loads((VSCODE_USER / "globalStorage/storage.json").read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None, {}
    profiles = {x.get("name"): x.get("location") for x in d.get("userDataProfiles") or [] if isinstance(x, dict)}
    return profiles, ((d.get("profileAssociations") or {}).get("workspaces") or {})


def profile_entry(location):
    """CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR in that profile's claudeCode.environmentVariables, or None."""
    try:
        cfg = json.loads((VSCODE_USER / "profiles" / location / "settings.json").read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    for e in cfg.get("claudeCode.environmentVariables") or []:
        if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("name") == "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR":
            return e.get("value")
    return None


CODE_CLI = os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_CODE") or "/Applications/Visual Studio Code.app/Contents/Resources/app/bin/code"


def code(*args, timeout=120):
    """(rc, output) of VS Code's CLI. The env goes without CLAUDE*: VS Code hands a CLI caller's
    environment to the window it opens (mainImpl.js `userEnv`, review 2026-10-16), and a session's
    CLAUDECODE or CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR has no place in the owner's window."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("CLAUDE")}
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([CODE_CLI] + list(args), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, env=env)
        return r.returncode, (r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else (r.stderr or r.stdout))
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        return 1, str(e)


def cmd_vscode(reg, label):
    """A VS Code profile named LABEL whose Claude Code panel runs on that login (the owner, 2026-10-16,
    orca-accounts D2: "add it to VScode profiles right now, so I can pick it manually when coding.
    Just don't break the open sessions please"). The work-account E7 route, built:
    - a missing profile is made by `code --profile LABEL --new-window` (VS Code has no other way):
      one new empty window, no folder — a folder given would be tied to LABEL for good;
    - CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR goes into that profile's own settings.json at once, before any extension,
      so its panel never starts on the default login; `claudeCode.environmentVariables` is
      machine-scoped, so it lives per profile (work-account P8). Never the default profile's file;
    - the default profile's extensions are copied in (a snapshot: a profile's sections cannot be
      shared from the CLI); later additions to the default do not follow.
    Idempotent; a backup before each settings write. Undo: delete the profile in VS Code."""
    a = row(reg, label)
    if a["provider"] != "claude":
        print(f"account: {label} is a {a['provider']} row — VS Code profiles are for Claude logins", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if is_default(a):
        print(f"account: {label} is the default dir — the default profile already runs on it", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not a["enabled"] or a.get("same_as"):
        print(f"account: {label} is {'disabled' if not a['enabled'] else 'on another row’s login'} — no profile for it", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not a["home"].is_dir():
        print(f"account: {label} has no dir here yet" + (f" — {orca_login_line(a)}" if via_orca(a) else ""), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if not os.path.exists(CODE_CLI):
        print(f"account: no VS Code CLI at {CODE_CLI}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    profiles, _ = vscode_state()
    if profiles is None:
        print(f"account: no VS Code state at {VSCODE_USER}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    made = False
    if label not in profiles:
        code("--profile", label, "--new-window")
        for _ in range(30):  # poll storage.json for the profile VS Code just made; never re-run the command
            profiles, _ = vscode_state()
            if label in (profiles or {}):
                break
            time.sleep(1)
        if label not in (profiles or {}):
            print(f"account: VS Code made no profile {label} within 30 s (storage.json)", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        made = True
        print(f"  made    VS Code profile {label} (one new, empty window)")
    try:
        flags = next((x.get("useDefaultFlags") or {} for x in json.loads((VSCODE_USER / "globalStorage/storage.json").read_text())
                      .get("userDataProfiles") or [] if isinstance(x, dict) and x.get("name") == label), {})
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        flags = {}
    if flags.get("settings"):
        print(f"account: profile {label} shares the default profile's settings — its panel would read the default's; "
              f"set its Settings to New in VS Code's Profiles editor, then run this again", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    p = VSCODE_USER / "profiles" / profiles[label] / "settings.json"
    try:
        text = p.read_text() if p.exists() else ""
        cfg = json.loads(text) if text.strip() else {}
        if not isinstance(cfg, dict):
            raise ValueError("not an object")
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        print(f"account: {p} is not plain JSON ({e}) — not edited; add CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']} to "
              f"claudeCode.environmentVariables there by hand", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    env = [e for e in cfg.get("claudeCode.environmentVariables") or [] if not (isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("name") == "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR")]
    want_env = env + [{"name": "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "value": str(a["home"])}]
    if cfg.get("claudeCode.environmentVariables") != want_env:
        if p.exists():
            shutil.copy2(p, p.with_name(f"settings.json.bak-{int(now())}"))
        cfg["claudeCode.environmentVariables"] = want_env
        p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = p.with_name(f"settings.json.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps(cfg, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
        os.replace(tmp, p)
        print(f"  set     {p}: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']}")
    else:
        print(f"  ok      VS Code profile {label}: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']}")
    rc_w, want = code("--list-extensions")
    rc_h, have = code("--profile", label, "--list-extensions")
    if rc_w or rc_h:
        print(f"  ⚠ skip  extensions: `code --list-extensions` failed ({(want if rc_w else have).strip()[:120]}) — "
              f"run `account vscode {label}` again", file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        for ext in sorted(set(want.split()) - set(have.split())):
            rc, out = code("--profile", label, "--install-extension", ext, timeout=300)
            print(f"  {'added ' if rc == 0 else '⚠ fail'}  extension {ext}" + ("" if rc == 0 else f": {out.strip()[:120]}"))
    print(f"  expect  a window in profile {label} (VS Code's Profiles menu): its Claude Code panel's first tier line "
          f"ends `— {label}`; every other window is unchanged" + ("; reload that new window once (extensions went in after it opened)" if made else ""))
    return 0


def workspace_folders(ws):
    """The folder paths of a .code-workspace (JSONC: comments and trailing commas tolerated by
    reading the "path" values, not the document)."""
    import re
    try:
        text = ws.read_text()
    except OSError:
        return []
    return [(ws.parent / m).resolve() for m in re.findall(r'"path"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"', text)]


def denied_workspaces(rows):
    """→ [(workspace, [denied keys], profile name)] for every .code-workspace under the projects folder
    (three levels) with a folder a row's deny list names."""
    profiles, assoc = vscode_state()
    by_loc = {v: k for k, v in (profiles or {}).items()}
    out = []
    projects = projects_dir()
    for pattern in ("*.code-workspace", "*/*.code-workspace", "*/*/*.code-workspace"):
        for ws in sorted(projects.glob(pattern)):
            if ".claude" in ws.parts:
                continue
            keys = sorted({d for f in workspace_folders(ws) for a in rows for d in [denied(a, f, routes=False)] if d})
            if keys:
                loc = assoc.get(ws.resolve().as_uri())
                out.append((ws, keys, "default" if loc in (None, "__default__profile__") else by_loc.get(loc, loc)))
    return out


def tenant_ties(reg):
    """→ [(label, level, text, fix)]: each tenant repo cloned here, and each workspace that opens one,
    against the VS Code profile VS Code ties it to. An untied one opens in the default profile, whose
    panel runs on the default dir, which the guard refuses it. `code --profile LABEL <path>` ties it
    for good (cmd_vscode's docstring); it opens a window, so it is the fix line, never run here."""
    profiles, assoc = vscode_state()
    if profiles is None or not reg.get("tenants"):
        return []
    by_loc = {v: k for k, v in profiles.items()}
    projects = projects_dir()
    out = []
    for name, entries in reg["tenants"].items():
        rows = {a["label"]: a for a in reg["accounts"] if a.get("tenant") == name and a["enabled"] and a["provider"] == "claude"}
        logins = list(rows)
        # a glob by fnmatch on the key, as denied() reads it (`*` spans `/`), never Path.glob (review 2026-10-21)
        clones = [g.parent for pat in ("*/.git", "*/*/.git", "*/*/*/.git") for g in projects.glob(pat)
                  if ".claude" not in g.parts]
        repos = set()
        for e in entries:
            for p in ([c for c in clones if fnmatch.fnmatchcase(c.relative_to(projects).as_posix(), e)]
                      if is_glob(e) else [projects / e]):
                if (p / ".git").exists():
                    repos.add(p.resolve())
        targets = sorted(repos)
        for pattern in ("*.code-workspace", "*/*.code-workspace", "*/*/*.code-workspace"):
            for ws in sorted(projects.glob(pattern)):
                if ".claude" not in ws.parts and any(f in repos or repos & set(f.parents) for f in workspace_folders(ws)):
                    targets.append(ws.resolve())
        for p in targets:
            loc = assoc.get(p.as_uri())
            prof = "default" if loc in (None, "__default__profile__") else by_loc.get(loc, loc)
            label = logins[0] if logins else "-"
            # a profile is the login's only when its panel carries the login's dir: `code --profile X`
            # makes a bare one when X is missing, so the profile comes first (review 2026-10-20)
            ready = [l for l in logins if profiles.get(l) and profile_entry(profiles[l]) == str(rows[l]["home"])]
            if prof in ready:
                out.append((prof, "ok", f"{p} opens on VS Code profile {prof}, {name}'s login"))
            elif logins and not ready:
                # info where no tenant login has a dir on this Mac: nothing here can tie it, and doctor
                # must be able to reach "every row ok" (review 2026-10-21, as the profile check above)
                here = any(rows[l]["home"].is_dir() for l in logins)
                out.append((label, "fix" if here else "info", f"{p} is {name}'s, and no VS Code profile runs its panel on {label}'s dir",
                            f"{BIN / 'account'} vscode {label}   (then tie {p} to it: doctor names the line)"))
            elif not logins:
                why, fix = no_login(name)
                out.append(("-", "fix", f"{p} is {name}'s, and {why}", fix))
            else:
                label = ready[0]
                out.append((label, "fix", f"{p} opens on VS Code profile {prof}, which is refused {name}'s repos",
                            f"{shlex.quote(CODE_CLI)} --profile {label} {shlex.quote(str(p))}   (opens it in a window, tied to {label} for good)"))
    return out


def attribution(label, days=7):
    """→ sessions usage-report attributed to label on this host in the last days (E6), or None
    when the attribution file does not exist yet."""
    try:
        from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader
        ur = SourceFileLoader("usage_report", str(core().tool("usage-report"))).load_module()   # plumb's; absent: None below (G18)
        f = ur.data_dir() / ur.this_host() / "accounts.jsonl"
    except Exception:
        return None
    if not f.is_file():
        return None
    n, t = 0, now()
    for line in f.read_text().splitlines():
        try:
            r = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if r.get("account") == label and t - (r.get("t") or 0) < days * 86400:
            n += 1
    return n


def credential_expiry(a):
    """→ expiresAt (epoch s) of the dir's .credentials.json (the always-on Mac's plaintext fallback, P1),
    or None where the login is in the keychain (the laptop — not read: it would prompt)."""
    f = (DEFAULT_DIR if is_default(a) else a["home"]) / ".credentials.json"
    try:
        d = json.loads(f.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    for v in d.values() if isinstance(d, dict) else []:
        if isinstance(v, dict) and v.get("expiresAt"):
            x = v["expiresAt"]
            return x / 1000 if x > 1e11 else x
    return None


def doctor_local(reg, logins, peer=None, record=True):
    """→ findings on this Mac: [{"mac", "label", "level": ok|fix|info, "text", "fix"?, "record"?}].
    peer: the ssh alias the laptop reaches this Mac by — fix lines come out in the ssh form."""
    mac, t, out = this_mac(), now(), []

    def f(label, level, text, fix=None, **kw):
        out.append(dict({"mac": mac, "label": label, "level": level, "text": text, "fix": fix}, **kw))

    claude_rows = [a for a in reg["accounts"] if a["provider"] == "claude"]
    for a in claude_rows:
        e = logins.get(a["label"])
        lab = a["label"]
        if e is None:
            f(lab, "fix" if a["enabled"] else "info", f"not in the logins file — add a row for {lab} to it")
            continue
        listed = mac in (e.get("macs") or [])
        if not listed:
            if a["home"].is_dir() and not is_default(a):
                f(lab, "info", f"{a['home']} exists but the logins file lists {lab} for {', '.join(e.get('macs') or []) or 'no Mac'}"
                  + (f" ({e['note']})" if e.get("note") else ""))
            continue
        if not a["home"].is_dir():
            f(lab, "fix", f"{a['home']} missing", wrap_peer(f"{BIN / 'account'} setup", peer))
            continue
        problems = 0
        _, email = login_of(a)  # the e-mail .claude.json remembers, not proof the login still works
        # Over ssh the peer's keychain is locked (work-account P1): a login held there, as Orca's GUI
        # sign-in is, reads logged out to every check here though its sessions run (an Orca work login, 2026-10-18)
        # SSH_CONNECTION too: the fix line for a failed peer leg runs `doctor --local` over ssh, no --peer
        blind = bool((peer or os.environ.get("SSH_CONNECTION")) and email and not ((DEFAULT_DIR if is_default(a) else a["home"]) / ".credentials.json").is_file())
        raw = auth_status(a) if email and not blind else None
        st = raw or {}
        if blind:
            f(lab, "info", "its login is in the keychain, which ssh cannot read — auth status and the live call not checked here")
        want = e.get("email")
        if not email:
            problems += 1
            f(lab, "fix", f"not signed in — sign in as {want or 'its own account'}", login_line(a, peer))
        elif not want:
            f(lab, "info", f"{email} recorded in the logins file" if record else f"{email}: the laptop records it",
              record={"email": email, "plan": st.get("subscriptionType"), "org_type": seat_of(a)})
        elif email.lower() != want.lower():
            problems += 1
            f(lab, "fix", f"signed in as {email}, the logins file says {want}", login_line(a, peer))
        if email and not blind and raw is None:   # a timeout or no JSON: no answer, not "logged out"
            problems += 1
            f(lab, "fix", "`claude auth status` gave no answer (a timeout, or not JSON)")
        elif email and not blind and not st.get("loggedIn"):
            problems += 1
            f(lab, "fix", f"{email} in {claude_json(a)}, but `claude auth status` says logged out", login_line(a, peer))
        if a.get("same_as"):
            problems += 1
            f(lab, "fix", f"same login as {a['same_as'][0]} ({a['same_as'][1]})", login_line(a, peer))
        lp = link_problems(a)
        if lp:
            problems += 1
            fix = (bootstrap_cmd() if any("does not exist" in x for x in lp)   # only it writes the file
                   else f"{BIN / 'account'} orca-sync" if via_orca(a) else f"{BIN / 'account'} add {lab} --no-login")
            f(lab, "fix", "links: " + "; ".join(lp), wrap_peer(fix, peer))   # `add` refuses an Orca row
        if not is_default(a):
            tr = subprocess.run(trust_argv(a, "--check"), capture_output=True, text=True,
                                env={**os.environ, "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR": str(a["home"])})
            if tr.returncode:
                problems += 1
                last = tr.stdout.strip().splitlines()[-1].strip() if tr.stdout.strip() else "trust unknown"
                f(lab, "fix", f"trust: {last}", wrap_peer(f"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']} {shlex.join(trust_argv(a))}", peer))
            if guard_state(a) != "wired":
                problems += 1
                f(lab, "fix", "guard: missing (hooks/account_guard.py, E4)",
                  wrap_peer(f"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']} {bootstrap_cmd()} --hooks", peer))
        # only where the script exists (wharf's; G16): wired before that, the command points at no file
        if (a["provider"] == "claude" and worktree_allow_present()
                and not worktree_hook(a)):
            problems += 1
            f(lab, "fix", "worktree hook: missing (hooks/worktree_allow.py), EnterWorktree can ask",
              # the dir spelled out, the default's too, so the run that wires every account dir
              # also gives that one its working directories and declared settings (review 2026-10-10)
              wrap_peer(f"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']} {bootstrap_cmd()} --hooks", peer))
        sl = settings_line(a)
        if sl and not sl.endswith(", 0 missing, 0 undeclared"):
            f(lab, "info", sl + f" — {BIN / 'claude-settings'} check lists each")
        # a blind row is not probed: over ssh get_usage answers its null "success", which limits()
        # would cache for every hook that reads --cached
        if a["enabled"] and email and not a.get("same_as") and not blind:
            lim, at = limits(a)
            # `auth status` reads the stored credential, never the server: on the always-on Mac it said
            # the primary ok over a dead token and a landing died on it (TODO, 2026-10-18). limits()
            # just probed unless its cache was fresh, so an old or missing reading is a failed live
            # call; and so is one with neither plan nor window, which get_usage answers "success"
            # with when no login reaches the server (an Orca work login there, 2026-10-18)
            answered = lim and (lim.get("plan") or any(lim.get(w) is not None for w in WINDOWS))
            # now(), not the doctor's start t: a probe that failed late falls back to a cache young against t
            if st.get("loggedIn") and (at is None or now() - at >= CACHE_S or not answered):
                problems += 1
                f(lab, "fix", "no answer to a live call (get_usage, two tries): the login is dead, or the network is down"
                  + (f"; last answer {when(at)}" if at else ""), login_line(a, peer))
            fb = (lim or {}).get("fable")
            prim = next((n for n, r in enumerate(reg["accounts"]) if r.get("primary")), None)
            if fb is False and prim is not None and reg["accounts"].index(a) > prim:
                # the owner, 2026-10-16: the Pro fallback back in the walk "after [the primary] — as the last resort"
                f(lab, "info", "fable: no — walked only after the primary, as the last resort")
            elif fb is False:
                problems += 1
                f(lab, "fix", "fable: no — not safe for main sessions (every one may spawn an architect)",
                  f"set \"enabled\": false on {lab} in {REGISTRY}, or build the fable-less rule (work-account section 2)")
            elif fb is None:
                f(lab, "info", "fable: unknown (no probe answer with limits[])")
        exp = credential_expiry(a)
        if exp is not None:
            left = exp - t
            if left < 0:
                f(lab, "info", f"access token past {when(exp)} — refreshed on the next call; the live call above says if the login is gone")
            elif left < EXPIRY_WARN_S:
                f(lab, "info", f"access token expires {when(exp)}")
        elif not is_default(a) or peer is None:
            pass  # the laptop keeps logins in the keychain: not read (it would prompt), said once below
        for rs in sorted(a["home"].glob("remote-settings*")) if not is_default(a) else sorted(DEFAULT_DIR.glob("remote-settings*")):
            f(lab, "info", f"{rs} exists — managed settings reached this dir (work-account P5); read it")
        if a["enabled"] and not is_default(a):
            n = attribution(lab)
            if n is None:
                f(lab, "info", "no usage attribution on this host yet (E6: sync.py writes it at session start)")
            else:
                f(lab, "info", f"{n} session(s) attributed in usage-report, last 7 days")
        if not problems:
            f(lab, "ok", f"{email or '?'}" + (f" ({e.get('plan')})" if e.get("plan") else "")
              + ("" if is_default(a) else f"; guard {guard_state(a)}"))
    # the VS Code profile per enabled secondary (D2-C), on a Mac with VS Code
    profiles, _ = vscode_state()
    # A dir signed in to another row's account is a second way into that seat which its deny list
    # and the guard do not cover: the Pro fallback's dir, parked, signed in to a work seat while
    # pickup still started sessions on it (review 2026-10-06). Every dir that exists, enabled or not.
    owner = {(e.get("email") or "").lower(): lab for lab, e in logins.items() if e and e.get("email")}
    for a in claude_rows:  # an enabled row's own login, until the logins file records it
        if a["enabled"] and a["home"].is_dir():
            owner.setdefault((login_of(a)[1] or "").lower(), a["label"])
    owner.pop("", None)
    for a in claude_rows:
        if not a["home"].is_dir():
            continue
        _, email = login_of(a)
        other = owner.get((email or "").lower())
        denies = any((r.get("repos") or {}).get("deny") for r in claude_rows if r["label"] == other)
        if email and other and other != a["label"] and denies:  # the primary's seat has no deny list to bypass
            f(a["label"], "fix", f"{a['home']} is signed in as {email}, which the logins file gives to {other} — "
              f"a second way into {other}'s seat that its deny list does not cover",
              wrap_peer(f"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']} claude auth logout   (after its sessions end)", peer)
              if not is_default(a) else "sign the default dir back in as its own account")
    if profiles is not None:
        for a in claude_rows:
            if not a["enabled"] or is_default(a) or mac not in ((logins.get(a["label"]) or {}).get("macs") or []):
                continue
            loc = profiles.get(a["label"])
            if not loc:
                # info, not fix: doctor must be able to reach "every row ok" (review 2026-10-06)
                f(a["label"], "info", f"no VS Code profile {a['label']} (work-account D2-C)", f"{BIN / 'account'} vscode {a['label']}")
            elif profile_entry(loc) != str(a["home"]):
                f(a["label"], "info", f"profile {a['label']} has no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR={a['home']}", f"{BIN / 'account'} vscode {a['label']}")
            else:
                f(a["label"], "ok", f"VS Code profile {a['label']} runs its panel on {a['home']}")
        deny_rows = [a for a in claude_rows if a["enabled"] and (a.get("repos") or {}).get("deny")]
        for ws, keys, prof in denied_workspaces(deny_rows):
            on = [a["label"] for a in deny_rows if a["label"] == prof]
            f(on[0] if on else "-", "fix" if on else "info",
              f"{ws} opens {', '.join(keys)} — profile {prof}" + (": a denied repo on that account" if on else ""),
              "open it with the default profile" if on else None)
        for t in tenant_ties(reg):
            f(*t)
    if peer is None and any(not is_default(a) for a in claude_rows if a["home"].is_dir()):
        f("-", "info", "credential expiry: the keychain is not read here (it would prompt)")
    known = {a["home"] for a in reg["accounts"]}
    for d in sorted(HOME.glob(".claude-*")):
        if d.is_dir() and d.absolute() not in known:
            f("-", "info", f"{d} is in no registry row")
    return out


def cmd_doctor(reg, local=False, as_json=False, peer=None):
    path, logins = load_logins()
    if not logins:
        print(f"account: no logins file at {path or 'the config dir (lighthouse/logins.json; Drive not mounted?)'} — nothing to check against")
        return 1
    found = doctor_local(reg, logins, peer=peer, record=not local)
    if not local:
        for host, alias in sorted(PEERS.items()):
            if host == this_mac():
                continue
            r = run_on_peer(host, ["doctor", "--local", "--json", "--peer", alias])
            try:
                peer_found = json.loads(r.stdout)
            except ValueError:
                peer_found = None
            if peer_found is None and r.returncode == 255:  # ssh's own failure, not the peer's doctor
                found.append({"mac": host, "label": "-", "level": "fix",
                              "text": f"unreachable over ssh ({(r.stderr or '').strip()[:120]})",
                              "fix": "off the LAN? /move --to-home, then run this again"})
            elif peer_found is None:  # reached, but its doctor said something else (no logins file there…)
                found.append({"mac": host, "label": "-", "level": "fix",
                              "text": f"its doctor answered: {(r.stdout or r.stderr or '').strip()[:300]}",
                              "fix": wrap_peer(f"PATH=$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH python3 {remote_account()} doctor --local", alias)})
            else:
                for x in peer_found:  # a missing dir there is built from here: the laptop's own path
                    if x.get("level") == "fix" and x.get("text", "").endswith(" missing"):
                        x["fix"] = f"{BIN / 'account'} setup --host {host}"
                found += peer_found
        recs = [x for x in found if x.get("record") and logins.get(x["label"]) is not None and not logins[x["label"]].get("email")]
        for x in recs:
            logins[x["label"]].update({k: v for k, v in x["record"].items() if v})
        if recs:
            save_logins(path, logins)
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(found, ensure_ascii=False))
        return 1 if any(x["level"] == "fix" for x in found) else 0
    mark = {"ok": "ok ", "fix": "⚠  ", "info": "-- "}
    for mac in dict.fromkeys(x["mac"] for x in found):
        print(f"{mac}:")
        for x in found:
            if x["mac"] != mac:
                continue
            print(f"  {mark[x['level']]} {x['label']}: {x['text']}")
            if x.get("fix"):
                print(f"      {x['fix']}")
    n = sum(1 for x in found if x["level"] == "fix")
    print(f"{n} to fix" if n else "every row ok")
    return 1 if n else 0


def run_on_peer(host, argv):
    """argv of bin/account run on the peer over ssh (ACCOUNT_SSH overrides the ssh command for
    tests) → CompletedProcess. The peer's own checkout runs it, at this tool's path from the home
    folder (remote_account)."""
    ssh = shlex.split(os.environ.get("ACCOUNT_SSH") or f"ssh -o ConnectTimeout=10 {PEERS[host]}")
    remote = f"PATH=$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH python3 {remote_account()} " + " ".join(shlex.quote(x) for x in argv)
    try:
        return subprocess.run(ssh + [remote], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=180)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(ssh, 255, "", str(e))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- main

def fable_word(lim):
    f = (lim or {}).get("fable")
    if f and (lim or {}).get("fable_week") is not None:
        return f"yes, its week {lim['fable_week']}%"
    return "yes" if f else ("no" if f is False else "unknown")


def limits_json(a, lim, at, t, repo=None):
    ok, why = judge(a, lim, t)
    d = denied(a, repo)
    if d:
        ok, why = False, off_reason(a, d)
    own = scope_login_of(repo)
    if own and own[1] != a["label"]:  # in `denied` too: dispatch's --force never crosses that
        ok, why, d = False, (f"scope {own[0]} runs on {own[1]}" if own[1] else own[0]), d or f"scope {own[0]}"
    if not a["enabled"]:  # only `limits --all --parked` lists one; never a pick. judge's reason kept: why no figures
        ok, why = False, f"parked (enabled: false in {REGISTRY.name})" + (f"; {why}" if why else "")
    return {"label": a["label"], "provider": a["provider"], "primary": bool(a.get("primary")), "enabled": a["enabled"],
            "here": a["home"].is_dir(), "home": str(a["home"]), "env": env_of(a), "limits": lim, "sampled": at, "eligible": ok, "why": why,
            "denied": d or None,  # --force never crosses a deny list (review 2026-10-06)
            "same_as": a["same_as"][0] if a.get("same_as") else None}


def main(argv):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="account", description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)
    p = sub.add_parser("pick")
    p.add_argument("--kind")
    p.add_argument("--provider")
    p.add_argument("--bound", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--repo")
    p.add_argument("--ship")
    p.add_argument("--no-pin", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--cached", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p = sub.add_parser("ships")
    p.add_argument("--release")
    p.add_argument("--pin", nargs=2, metavar=("KEY", "LABEL"))
    p = sub.add_parser("orca-sync")
    p.add_argument("--off", action="store_true")
    p = sub.add_parser("orca")
    p.add_argument("what", choices=("on", "off", "default"))
    p.add_argument("label", nargs="?")
    p = sub.add_parser("vscode")
    p.add_argument("label")
    p = sub.add_parser("limits")
    p.add_argument("label", nargs="?")
    p.add_argument("--all", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--parked", action="store_true", help="with --all: the rows accounts.json parks too, never an `orca off` one (the widget)")
    p.add_argument("--orca", action="store_true", help="with --all: a report-only row per other agent Orca reads limits for (the widget)")
    p.add_argument("--repo")
    p.add_argument("--cached", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p = sub.add_parser("env")
    p.add_argument("label")
    p = sub.add_parser("run")
    p.add_argument("label")
    p.add_argument("claude_args", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER)
    p = sub.add_parser("exhausted")
    p.add_argument("--window", required=True, choices=WINDOWS)
    p.add_argument("--resets-at", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--used", type=float)
    p.add_argument("--session")
    sub.add_parser("check")
    sub.add_parser("which")
    p = sub.add_parser("allowed")
    p.add_argument("path")
    p = sub.add_parser("add")
    p.add_argument("label")
    p.add_argument("--no-login", action="store_true")
    p = sub.add_parser("setup")
    p.add_argument("--host", choices=sorted(PEERS))
    p.add_argument("--no-record", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--peer", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)  # set by --host: login lines in the ssh -t form
    p = sub.add_parser("doctor")
    p.add_argument("--local", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--peer", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if args.cmd == "exhausted":  # the statusline's call: needs no registry, only this dir
        ra = args.resets_at
        record_exhaustion(current_home(), args.window, float(ra) if ra.replace(".", "", 1).isdigit() else ra,
                          args.used, args.session)
        return 0
    reg = load_registry()
    if args.cmd in ("env", "run") and via_orca(row(reg, args.label)) and not row(reg, args.label).get("synced"):
        print(f"account: {args.label}'s login is not in Orca here — {orca_login_line(row(reg, args.label))}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if args.cmd == "env":
        for k, v in env_of(row(reg, args.label)).items():
            print(f"{k}={shlex.quote(v)}")
        return 0
    if args.cmd == "run":  # `cc i` (shell/cc.zsh): a hand-picked login, replacing `cswap switch`
        a = row(reg, args.label)
        if not a["enabled"]:
            print(f"account: {a['label']} is disabled in {REGISTRY}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        if a["provider"] != "claude":  # it would exec claude on the default login with only CODEX_HOME set
            print(f"account: {a['label']} is a {a['provider']} login — run starts claude only", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        cwd = os.getcwd()
        r = routed_off(a, cwd)  # a tenant's repo is off every other row, the default dir's too
        d = r[0] if r else None if is_default(a) else (denied(a, cwd) or holds_denied(a, cwd))
        if d:
            why = off_line(a, d) or f"{d} is off {a['label']} (accounts.json deny list)"
            print(f"account: {why}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        # An inherited token outranks the config dir's login (autofinish-run's KEEP_CLAUDE), so a
        # session started from inside another would silently spend that token's seat.
        e = {k: v for k, v in start_env(a).items() if k != "CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"}
        try:
            os.execvpe("claude", ["claude"] + args.claude_args, e)
        except OSError as err:
            print(f"account: cannot start claude: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
    if args.cmd == "check":
        return cmd_check(reg)
    if args.cmd == "which":  # the label of the dir this process runs under (dispatch --next inherits it)
        # Only an enabled secondary: a default-dir session's next step stays `auto`, which keeps
        # pick's "the primary runs it rather than nothing"; a named primary would turn a full or
        # unprobed window into FAILED, and a parked row into "disabled" (review 2026-10-06).
        for a in reg["accounts"]:
            if (a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"] == current_home() and not is_default(a)
                    and a.get("enabled", True)):
                print(a["label"])
                return 0
        return 1
    if args.cmd == "allowed":  # the guard's question: this process's dir, the path's repo
        here = current_home()
        for a in reg["accounts"]:
            if a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"] == here and is_default(a):
                r = routed_off(a, args.path)   # the default dir denies nothing but another tenant's repo
                if r:
                    print(off_line(a, r[0]))
                    return 1
            if a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"] == here and not is_default(a):
                d = denied(a, args.path)
                whose = "ORCA_ROW's deny list" if a.get("implicit") else "accounts.json deny list"
                if d:
                    print(off_line(a, d) or f"{d} is off {a['label']} ({whose})")
                    return 1
                h = holds_denied(a, args.path)
                if h:   # a folder holding a denied repo: its sessions would load what the list keeps out
                    print(f"{args.path} is off {a['label']}: it holds {h} ({whose}); start the session inside a repo")
                    return 1
        if any(a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"] == here for a in reg["accounts"]):
            return 0
        # A login dir under Orca's data that no row names (the owner, 2026-10-17: "don't expose them to
        # employer login … [the primary and the Pro fallback] only"; reviews of docs/orca-hosts-2026-10-17.md).
        # Two kinds, told apart by the e-mail its .claude.json is signed in as, the way orca-sync
        # tells them: a registry row's own login added to Orca (orca-sync never records it — "that
        # row decides"), which gets that row's deny list; and anything else — a fresh `orca account
        # add`, a sync that failed — which gets ORCA_ROW's, as a synced Orca login does, so an
        # employer seat never reaches the vault through a missed sync. The whole of ORCA_DATA,
        # since orca_home_of looks there, not only today's claude-profiles/ layout.
        try:
            here.resolve().relative_to(ORCA_DATA.resolve())
        except (ValueError, OSError):
            # no row names this dir (a registry without the default dir's row, a stray dir): it is no
            # tenant's, so a tenant's repo is off it (review 2026-10-20); anything else stays open
            r = routed_off({"label": "this login (no row)", "routed": routes_for(reg, {})}, args.path)
            if r:
                print(off_line({"label": "this login (no row)", "routed": routes_for(reg, {})}, r[0]))
                return 1
            return 0
        email = (signed_in_as(here) or "").lower()
        owner = None
        if email:
            try:
                _, logins = load_logins()
            except SystemExit:  # an unreadable logins file: the rows' own logins still answer
                logins = {}
            by_label = {a["label"]: a for a in reg["accounts"]}
            for lab, e in logins.items():   # the first Claude row the logins file gives this e-mail
                if owner is None and isinstance(e, dict) and (e.get("email") or "").lower() == email \
                        and lab in by_label and by_label[lab]["provider"] == "claude":
                    owner = by_label[lab]
            for a in reg["accounts"]:
                if owner is None and not via_orca(a) and a["provider"] == "claude" and a["home"] != here \
                        and a["home"].is_dir() and (login_of(a)[1] or "").lower() == email:
                    owner = a
        if owner is not None:
            d, h = denied(owner, args.path), holds_denied(owner, args.path)
            if d:
                print(off_line(owner, d) or f"{d} is off {owner['label']}, whose login this Orca dir is (accounts.json deny list)")
                return 1
            if h:
                print(f"{args.path} is off {owner['label']}, whose login this Orca dir is: it holds {h}; "
                      f"start the session inside a repo")
                return 1
            return 0
        common = core()
        unsynced = common.orca_row("unsynced")
        unsynced["routed"] = routes_for(reg, unsynced)   # no row names it, so no tenant either
        d = denied(unsynced, args.path) or holds_denied(unsynced, args.path)
        if d:
            print(off_line(unsynced, d) or f"{d} is off this Orca login, which no row names yet (ORCA_ROW's deny list; "
                  f"`account orca-sync` records it)")
            return 1
        return 0
    if args.cmd == "add":
        return cmd_add(reg, args.label, not args.no_login)
    if args.cmd == "vscode":
        return cmd_vscode(reg, args.label)
    if args.cmd == "orca-sync":
        rc = cmd_orca_sync(reg, args.off)
        return orca_codex_sync(reg, args.off) or rc
    if args.cmd == "orca":
        return cmd_orca_switch(reg, args.what, args.label)
    if args.cmd == "ships":
        if args.pin:
            row(reg, args.pin[1])  # exits on an unknown label
            with ships_lock():
                ships = load_ships()
                ships[args.pin[0]] = {"label": args.pin[1], "at": int(now())}
                write_json(SHIPS, ships)
            print(f"{args.pin[0]}: pinned to {args.pin[1]}")
            return 0
        if args.release:
            with ships_lock():
                ships = load_ships()
                gone = ships.pop(args.release, None)
                write_json(SHIPS, ships)
            print(f"{args.release}: {'released from ' + gone['label'] if gone else 'had no pin'}")
            return 0
        ships = load_ships()
        for k, v in sorted(ships.items()):
            print(f"{k}: {v['label']} (last used {when(v['at'])})")
        return 0
    if args.cmd == "doctor":
        return cmd_doctor(reg, args.local, args.json, args.peer)
    if args.cmd == "setup":
        if args.host:
            r = run_on_peer(args.host, ["setup", "--no-record", "--peer", PEERS[args.host]])
            if r.returncode == 255:
                print(f"account: {args.host} unreachable over ssh ({(r.stderr or '').strip()[:120]}) — off the LAN? /move --to-home")
                return 1
            sys.stdout.write(r.stdout)
            sys.stderr.write(r.stderr)
            return r.returncode
        return cmd_setup(reg, record=not args.no_record, peer=args.peer)
    if args.cmd == "limits":
        rows = reg["accounts"] if args.all or not args.label else [row(reg, args.label)]
        if args.label and not args.all and not rows[0]["enabled"]:
            sys.exit(f"account: {args.label} is disabled in {REGISTRY}")
        # --parked keeps the rows accounts.json parks (`enabled: false`): the desktop widget draws
        # them — a login the owner runs by hand still spends its week (a work login, 2026-10-18). An `orca off`
        # login stays hidden: that is how a login leaves the widget. The walk, the tier check and
        # usage-report read `--all` alone and never see either.
        rows = [a for a in rows if a["enabled"] or (args.parked and a["parked"])]
        t = now()
        out = []
        for a in rows:
            lim, at = limits(a, cached=args.cached)
            out.append(limits_json(a, lim, at, t, args.repo))
        if args.orca and (args.all or not args.label):
            out += orca_reported({a["label"] for a in reg["accounts"]}, cached=args.cached)
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps(out, ensure_ascii=False))
            return 0
        for o in out:
            lim = o["limits"]
            if o.get("report"):  # Orca's figure for an agent no row names: no plan or org of ours to add
                figs = ([f"{n} {lim[w]}% (resets {when(lim[w + '_resets'])})" for n, w in (("5h", "five_hour"), ("week", "seven_day"))
                         if lim.get(w) is not None]
                        + [f"{b['name']} {b['percent']}%" + (f" (resets {when(b['resets'])})" if b.get("resets") else "")
                           for b in (lim.get("buckets") or []) + (lim.get("windows") or [])]) if lim else []
                print(f"{o['label']} (Orca): {'; '.join(figs) if figs else 'no reading'}; {o['why']}")
                continue
            if not lim:
                print(f"{o['label']}: {'no cached probe' if args.cached else 'probe failed'}")
                continue
            # R5: the probe's plan string beside the dir's own, until the lesson says which to trust
            try:
                org = (json.loads(claude_json(row(reg, o["label"])).read_text()).get("oauthAccount") or {}).get("organizationType")
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                org = None
            print(f"{o['label']}: "
                  + (f"5h {lim['five_hour']}% (resets {when(lim['five_hour_resets'])}), " if lim.get("five_hour") is not None else "no 5-hour window, ")
                  + (f"week {lim['seven_day']}% (resets {when(lim['seven_day_resets'])})" if lim.get("seven_day") is not None else "no week window")
                  + f"; plan {lim.get('plan')} / {org}; "
                  f"fable: {fable_word(lim)}; "
                  f"{'eligible' if o['eligible'] else 'not eligible'}: {o['why']}")
        return 0
    if not REGISTRY.is_file():  # exit 4: no registry on this machine — callers start as before
        print(f"account: no registry at {REGISTRY}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 4
    try:
        if args.ship and args.bound:
            sys.exit("account: --ship and --bound do not go together (bound work is the primary's, pinned already)")
        if args.ship:
            a, why, skipped = pick_for_ship(reg, args.ship, args.kind, args.provider, args.cached, args.repo,
                                            pin=not (args.cached or args.no_pin))
        else:
            a, why, skipped = pick(reg, args.kind, args.provider, args.bound, args.cached, args.repo)
    except Short as s:
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps({"account": None, "skipped": s.args[0]}, ensure_ascii=False))
        else:
            print("account: no account may run this now —", file=sys.stderr)
            for label, w in s.args[0]:
                print(f"  {label}: {w}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps({"account": a["label"], "primary": bool(a.get("primary")), "provider": a["provider"],
                          "env": env_of(a), "reason": why, "skipped": skipped}, ensure_ascii=False))
    else:
        print(f"{a['label']}: {why}")
        for label, w in skipped:
            print(f"  skipped {label}: {w}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
